  'Blank A is distance 0 on the diagonal and 2 between nodes 6 and 7, because the shortest path is 6-4-7. Blank B is 0.41: node 2''s distances add up to 1+2+2+3+3+1+3+3+4 = 22, and 9 / 22 ≈ 0.41. In bfs, the start node''s distance is set first with d[s] ← 0 (blank C). Each newly found neighbour gets d[w] ← d[v] + 1 (blank D), one edge further than the node it was reached from. On the first call, every one of the 10 nodes is enqueued once and dequeued once, so line α runs 10 times (E). Line β runs once for each neighbour of each dequeued node, which is the sum of the degrees = 2 × 14 edges = 28 (F). Line γ enqueues every node except the start node, so it runs 9 times (G).',
