  'Blank A is "%d", pol[0]: a degree-0 polynomial is just its constant term. Blank B is pol[i], i, because the loop variable i is both the array index and the power of the term. In print_coef, a coefficient of 1 at power 0 must print "+1" (blank C), because that term cannot be the leading one. A coefficient of -1 always prints "-" first (blank D), and "1" is added only when the power is 0. print_star prints * only when pow > 0 (blank E) and the coefficient is not ±1. With the modified print_xpow, every power above 0 prints as x^n, so x prints as x^1 and the output is -7*x^4-x^2+x^1+11; zero terms are still skipped and the constant term has no x. For {0, 0, 0} with deg 2, every coefficient is 0, so nothing is printed except the final newline.',
