  'The correct equality is (A ∩ C) ∪ (B ∩ C) = (A ∪ B) ∩ C. This is the distributive law: intersection distributes over union, so factoring out the common set C gives the same set on both sides. The other choices fail for simple counterexamples, for example (A ∪ B) ∩ C is not equal to (A ∪ C) ∩ (B ∪ C), which is the distributive form of (A ∩ B) ∪ C instead.',
