(
  '2021-october',
  'A',
  9,
  'Q9. When the function M(n) is defined as shown below, what is the value of M(97)?

M(n) = n − 10 (n > 100)
M(n) = M(M(n + 11)) (n ≤ 100)',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q9. When the function M(n) is defined as shown below, what is the value of M(97)?\n\nM(n) = n − 10 (n > 100)\nM(n) = M(M(n + 11)) (n ≤ 100)"}]'::jsonb,
  '["2021-october-a-q09-figure1.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"81"},{"label":"b","text":"86"},{"label":"c","text":"91"},{"label":"d","text":"96"}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'M(97) = 91. M(97) = M(M(108)) = M(98), then M(98) = M(M(109)) = M(99), M(99) = M(M(110)) = M(100), and M(100) = M(M(111)) = M(101) = 101 − 10 = 91. This is McCarthy''s 91 function, which returns 91 for every n ≤ 100.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2021A, FE, Subject-A, Q9)',
