(
  '2021-october',
  'A',
  68,
  'Q68. Company T sells three (3) products: A, B, and C. Currently, products A, B, and C have 10,000, 20,000, and 80,000 purchasers every month, respectively. There is a plan to change the product line and sell four (4) new products, P, Q, R, and S, from next year. Accordingly, a trial calculation was made on the proportion of existing customers that will purchase the new products and the number of new customers. Which of the following is an appropriate conclusion from the trial calculation? Here, numerical values up to one (1) decimal point in each line of the table below represent the proportion of people who will change to the new product from the corresponding old product.',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q68. Company T sells three (3) products: A, B, and C. Currently, products A, B, and C have 10,000, 20,000, and 80,000 purchasers every month, respectively. There is a plan to change the product line and sell four (4) new products, P, Q, R, and S, from next year. Accordingly, a trial calculation was made on the proportion of existing customers that will purchase the new products and the number of new customers. Which of the following is an appropriate conclusion from the trial calculation? Here, numerical values up to one (1) decimal point in each line of the table below represent the proportion of people who will change to the new product from the corresponding old product."}]'::jsonb,
  '["2021-october-a-q68-figure1.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"Among the purchasers of product A, 1,000 people are estimated to purchase the new\n product Q."},{"label":"b","text":"Of the prospective purchasers of the new product P, 50% are estimated to be purchasers\n of product A."},{"label":"c","text":"The number of new customers of the new product S are estimated to be fewer than the\n number of people who will purchase the new product S among the purchasers of product\n C."},{"label":"d","text":"The purchasers of product B are estimated to purchase any one (1) of the new products\n P, Q, R, and S."}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'The correct conclusion is that new customers of product S (23,000) are fewer than the product C purchasers expected to switch to S (80,000 × 0.3 = 24,000). The other choices fail: A purchasers moving to Q are 10,000 × 0.3 = 3,000, not 1,000; A purchasers make up 5,000 of the 15,000 existing customers of P, about 33%, not 50%; and B''s proportions sum to 0.9, so not every B purchaser buys a new product.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'business-strategy'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2021A, FE, Subject-A, Q68)',
  ARRAY[26]::int[],
  'manual_review',
