  'A DHCP request is a broadcast, and routers do not forward broadcasts, so the DHCP server only hears clients on its own network 172.16.2.0/24 (A). A relay agent on Router A turns the request into a unicast addressed directly to the DHCP server, 172.16.2.10 (B). PC-B#1 at 172.16.2.50 has the default gateway 172.16.1.1, which is Router A''s interface on a different network, so it cannot reach other networks: the host received an incorrect gateway address from the DHCP server (C). The gateway handed out for Network B must be Router B''s interface on that network, 172.16.2.1 (D). On the firewall, the first rule maps mail transfer from the Internet to the mail server, so its service is SMTP (E), and DMZ servers reach the Internet through address translation, so the translation is NAT (F).',
