  '[{"type":"text","page":5,"bbox":[65.22,456.72,437.88,488.17],"text":"Q9. Which of the following is an appropriate description of a binary search?"},{"type":"text","page":5,"bbox":[84.12,492.89,357.05,506.17],"text":"a) A binary search is always faster than a linear search."},{"type":"text","page":5,"bbox":[84.12,510.89,525.36,560.17],"text":"b) In a binary search, searching starts from the beginning of the data.\nc) The data upon which the binary search is to be performed must be sorted.\nd) The number of comparisons that need to be performed during the search is proportional"},{"type":"text","page":5,"bbox":[65.22,564.89,328.8,595.09],"text":"to log₂N, where N is the number of data items."}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"A binary search is always faster than a linear search."},{"label":"b","text":"In a binary search, searching starts from the beginning of the data."},{"label":"c","text":"The data upon which the binary search is to be performed must be sorted."},{"label":"d","text":"The number of comparisons that need to be performed during the search is proportional\n to log₂N, where N is the number of data items."}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'The correct choice is that the data must be sorted before a binary search can be used. Binary search compares the target with the middle element and discards half of the range each time, which only works if the data is in order. It starts from the middle, not the beginning, and for very small data sets it is not always faster than a linear search.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
