(
  '2022-october',
  'A',
  8,
  'Q8. When a two-dimensional array A(5,5) is mapped to computer memory (i.e. a one-dimensional array) in row-major (row-directional) order or column-major (column-directional) order, how many elements occupy the same memory addresses in both cases?
 Here, the first element A(1,1) is mapped to the same starting memory address in either case.',
  '[{"type":"text","page":4,"bbox":[65.22,409.8,530.12,423.25],"text":"Q8.  When a two-dimensional array A(5,5) is mapped to computer memory (i.e. a one-"},{"type":"text","page":4,"bbox":[85.74,427.85,533.14,477.13],"text":"dimensional array) in row-major (row-directional) order or column-major (column-\ndirectional) order, how many elements occupy the same memory addresses in both cases?\nHere, the first element A(1,1) is mapped to the same starting memory address in either case."},{"type":"text","page":4,"bbox":[65.22,500.93,464.1,549.25],"text":"a) 2\nb) 3\nc) 4\nd) 5"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"2"},{"label":"b","text":"3"},{"label":"c","text":"4"},{"label":"d","text":"5"}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'Five elements share the same address. In row-major order A(i,j) is at offset 5(i−1) + (j−1), and in column-major order it is at 5(j−1) + (i−1); these are equal only when i = j. The diagonal elements A(1,1) to A(5,5) therefore stay in place, giving 5.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'data-structures'),
  'medium',
