(
  '2022-october',
  'A',
  9,
  'Q9. Which of the following characterizes the hash method in table search?',
  '[{"type":"text","page":4,"bbox":[65.22,554.76,435.46,585.13],"text":"Q9.  Which of the following characterizes the hash method in table search?"},{"type":"text","page":4,"bbox":[83.22,589.68,407.75,675.13],"text":"a) It determines the storage location using a function of a key.\nb) It is a method that avoids collisions among storage locations.\nc) It is one of the methods that use a binary tree.\nd) It takes time almost proportional to the table size for searching."},{"type":"code","page":4,"bbox":[65.22,794.45,312.66,807.79],"text":"– 4 –"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"It determines the storage location using a function of a key."},{"label":"b","text":"It is a method that avoids collisions among storage locations."},{"label":"c","text":"It is one of the methods that use a binary tree."},{"label":"d","text":"It takes time almost proportional to the table size for searching."}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'The hash method computes the storage location directly by applying a hash function to the key, so a search usually takes constant time regardless of table size. It does not avoid collisions (different keys can hash to the same location and must be handled), it does not use a binary tree, and its search time is not proportional to the table size.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'data-structures'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2022A, FE, Subject-A, Q9)',
