(
  '2022-october',
  'A',
  18,
  'Q18. In an OS, task scheduling is performed by the priority order method. The table below shows the priorities for three (3) tasks A to C and the execution sequence and execution time of the CPU and I/O device for the tasks when each task is executed individually. What is the idle time of the CPU in milliseconds from the time when the three (3) tasks are set to the ready state to the time when the execution of all of the tasks is finished? Here, there is one (1) CPU, the CPU consists of one (1) core, there is no I/O contention, and no overhead of the OS is considered. In the table below, the numbers in parentheses indicate the execution time for each operation.

Tasks | Priority | Execution sequence and execution time (in milliseconds) when each task is executed individually
A | High | CPU(3) → I/O(5) → CPU(2)
B | Medium | CPU(2) → I/O(6) → CPU(2)
C | Low | CPU(1) → I/O(5) → CPU(1)',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q18. In an OS, task scheduling is performed by the priority order method. The table below shows the priorities for three (3) tasks A to C and the execution sequence and execution time of the CPU and I/O device for the tasks when each task is executed individually. What is the idle time of the CPU in milliseconds from the time when the three (3) tasks are set to the ready state to the time when the execution of all of the tasks is finished? Here, there is one (1) CPU, the CPU consists of one (1) core, there is no I/O contention, and no overhead of the OS is considered. In the table below, the numbers in parentheses indicate the execution time for each operation.\n\nTasks | Priority | Execution sequence and execution time (in milliseconds) when each task is executed individually\nA | High | CPU(3) → I/O(5) → CPU(2)\nB | Medium | CPU(2) → I/O(6) → CPU(2)\nC | Low | CPU(1) → I/O(5) → CPU(1)"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"2"},{"label":"b","text":"3"},{"label":"c","text":"4"},{"label":"d","text":"5"}]'::jsonb,
  'b',
  '{"value":"b"}'::jsonb,
  'The CPU is idle for 3 ms. A runs from 0 to 3 ms and then does I/O until 8, B runs from 3 to 5 and then does I/O until 11, and C runs from 5 to 6 and then does I/O until 11. The CPU is idle from 6 to 8 until A returns and runs from 8 to 10. It is idle again from 10 to 11, after which B runs from 11 to 13 and C from 13 to 14. The total idle time is 2 + 1 = 3 ms.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'operating-systems'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2022A, FE, Subject-A, Q18)',
  ARRAY[7]::int[],
