(
  '2022-october',
  'A',
  68,
  'Q68. In a process that aims to produce ideal technology, technology follows an evolutionary
 process that progresses from the introduction stage, growth stage, maturity stage, and decline
 stage. Then, it transitions to the next technological phase. Which of the following is used to
 represent this technological evolution process?',
  '[{"type":"text","page":23,"bbox":[65.22,67.8,533.11,135.13],"text":"Q68.\nIn a process that aims to produce ideal technology, technology follows an evolutionary\nprocess that progresses from the introduction stage, growth stage, maturity stage, and decline\nstage. Then, it transitions to the next technological phase. Which of the following is used to\nrepresent this technological evolution process?"},{"type":"text","page":23,"bbox":[65.22,158.93,428.04,207.25],"text":"a) Bathtub curve\nb) Demand curve\nc) Learning curve\nd) Technology S-curve"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"Bathtub curve"},{"label":"b","text":"Demand curve"},{"label":"c","text":"Learning curve"},{"label":"d","text":"Technology S-curve"}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'The technology S-curve shows that a technology''s performance improves slowly when it is introduced, then rapidly during growth, then levels off at maturity, after which a new technology with its own S-curve takes over. A bathtub curve shows failure rates over time, a learning curve shows cost falling with experience, and a demand curve relates price to quantity.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'business-strategy'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2022A, FE, Subject-A, Q68)',
  ARRAY[23]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q68. In a process that aims to produce ideal technology, technology follows an evolutionary
 process that progresses from the introduction stage, growth stage, maturity stage, and decline
 stage. Then, it transitions to the next technological phase. Which of the following is used to
 represent this technological evolution process?

 a) Bathtub curve b) Demand curve
 c) Learning curve d) Technology S-curve',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2022-october/a/questions.pdf","pages":[23]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2022-october/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2022-october',
  'A',
  69,
  'Q69. When trial production is performed for components to be loaded into electrical appliances, which of the following is the least expensive development method? Here, the construction time of each process is six (6) months for the creation process, three (3) months for the modification process, and two (2) months for the evaluation process. The cost per one (1) person-month is 6,000 dollars for the creation process and 10,000 dollars for each of the modification and evaluation processes. Costs other than the purchase cost, outsourcing cost, and person-month cost shall not be considered.

Each choice lists: development method; purchase cost (dollars); outsourcing cost (dollars); number of persons per month (persons) for the creation, modification, and evaluation processes.',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q69. When trial production is performed for components to be loaded into electrical appliances, which of the following is the least expensive development method? Here, the construction time of each process is six (6) months for the creation process, three (3) months for the modification process, and two (2) months for the evaluation process. The cost per one (1) person-month is 6,000 dollars for the creation process and 10,000 dollars for each of the modification and evaluation processes. Costs other than the purchase cost, outsourcing cost, and person-month cost shall not be considered.\n\nEach choice lists: development method; purchase cost (dollars); outsourcing cost (dollars); number of persons per month (persons) for the creation, modification, and evaluation processes."}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"Purchasing samples and modifying them in-house. Purchase cost: 200,000, Outsourcing cost: 0, Creation process: 0, Modification process: 4, Evaluation process: 1"},{"label":"b","text":"Outsourcing in batches. Purchase cost: 0, Outsourcing cost: 350,000, Creation process: 0, Modification process: 0, Evaluation process: 0"},{"label":"c","text":"Modifying in-house assets. Purchase cost: 0, Outsourcing cost: 0, Creation process: 0, Modification process: 10, Evaluation process: 3"},{"label":"d","text":"Creating new products in-house. Purchase cost: 0, Outsourcing cost: 0, Creation process: 10, Modification process: 0, Evaluation process: 2"}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'Purchasing samples and modifying them in-house is cheapest at 340,000 dollars. It costs 200,000 + (4 persons × 3 months × 10,000) + (1 × 2 × 10,000) = 200,000 + 120,000 + 20,000 = 340,000. Outsourcing costs 350,000. Modifying in-house assets costs 10 × 3 × 10,000 + 3 × 2 × 10,000 = 360,000, and creating new products costs 10 × 6 × 6,000 + 2 × 2 × 10,000 = 400,000.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'finance'),
  'medium',
