(
  '2022-october',
  'B',
  1,
  'Q1. Read the following description of security in an Internet-based order management system,
 and then answer Subquestions 1 through 4.

A manufacturer named Company M develops an Internet-based order management system.
 Company M sells its products to wholesalers. Since wholesalers also use the order
 management system, any security defects may result in damage to not only Company M but
 also the wholesalers. Therefore, Company M requested Company S, which provides security
 analytic services, to perform a vulnerability analysis of the order management system.

[Order management system]
 The order management system’s application (hereinafter, the application) runs on a Web
 server. Orders, shipments, and other information are stored in an order information database
 that runs on the database server. The application is used to reference and update the
 information. Files that can be downloaded to or files that were uploaded from wholesaler’s
 PCs are stored on a disk that is connected to the Web server. Figure 1 shows the configuration
 of the order management system.

Wholesaler’s internal network (partial)

FW3 The Internet

Whole- Whole-
 … FW1 RPS saler’s PC saler’s PC

FW2

FW: Firewall Web Database Internal … Internal
 RPS: Reverse proxy server server server PC PC

Company M’s internal network (partial)

Figure 1 Configuration of the order management system

A digital certificate is configured on the RPS. The operator of the wholesaler who utilizes
 the order management system accesses the application via RPS from a browser on the
 wholesaler’s PC, and logs in using a user ID and password on the login screen. At that point,
 the browser on the wholesaler’s PC uses HTTP over TLS (hereinafter, HTTPS) for
 communication, and the RPS uses the digital certificate to convert the protocol from HTTPS
 to HTTP.

[Result of the vulnerability analysis by Company S]
In the application, measures have been taken against ***A*** that makes the database
server execute unexpected operations to gain unauthorized access to the database. However,
the vulnerability analysis by Company S reveals multiple security vulnerabilities that require
measures in the application. Table 1 shows an excerpt of the findings by Company S.

Table 1 Findings by Company S (excerpt)

Finding Cause
An operator of a wholesaler can access
another wholesaler’s order and shipping
information.
An operator of a wholesaler can download (i) An issue exists in the download
arbitrary files on the Web server. processing of files in the application.
Since there is a ***B*** vulnerability by
which a script can be embedded in a Web
page by an attacker, there is a possibility
that an operator of a wholesaler can be led
to another Web site that steals the user ID
and password.
 (ii) When an operator of a wholesaler
 logs in, the user ID is not locked even
 when the operator repeatedly enters
 wrong passwords.
 Note: Shaded parts are not shown.

Company M took the measures to address the underlined sections (i) and (ii) in Table 1.
Furthermore, Company M took measures to address the other findings by Company S,
including security education for operators to prevent ***C*** incidents that take
advantage of human errors to gain unauthorized access, and decided to launch the order
management system.

Subquestion 1
 The sections of the communication routes in Figure 1 are numbered 1 through 5 as shown in
 Table 2. From the answer group below, select the correct answer that represents all of the
 communication routes that use HTTPS for communication when the wholesaler’s PC
 accesses the Web server.

Table 2 Communication routes between devices

Route number Communication route
 1 Between each wholesaler’s PC and FW3
 2 Between FW3 and FW1
 3 Between FW1 and RPS
 4 Between FW1 and FW2
 5 Between FW2 and the Web server

Answer group
 a) 1 b) 1, 2, 3
 c) 1, 2, 3, 4 d) 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
 e) 2, 3, 4 f) 2, 3, 4, 5
 g) 3, 4

Subquestion 2
 From the answer group below, select the appropriate answer to be inserted in each blank
 _______ in the description.

Answer group for A through C
 a) brute force attacks b) cross-site scripting
 c) dictionary attacks d) directory traversal
 e) DoS attacks f) port scanning
 g) social engineering h) SQL injection

Subquestion 3
 From the answer group below, select the answer that is appropriate as a measure to address
 the underlined section (i) in Table 1.

Answer group
 a) Making the user specify only the name of the file to be downloaded, and if the file
 exists in the folder set for each wholesaler, performing download processing.
 b) Making the user specify the file to be downloaded by using a relative path, and if the
 file exists, performing download processing.
 c) Making the user specify the file to be downloaded by using an absolute path, and if
 the file exists, performing download processing.
 d) On the browser on the wholesaler’s PC, displaying the structure of all folders and the
 files on the Web server, making the user specify the file to be downloaded, and
 performing download processing.

Subquestion 4
 From the answer group below, select the answer that is appropriate as a security incident that
 can be caused by the vulnerability in the underlined section (ii) in Table 1.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q1. Read the following description of security in an Internet-based order management system,\n and then answer Subquestions 1 through 4."},{"type":"context","text":"A manufacturer named Company M develops an Internet-based order management system.\n Company M sells its products to wholesalers. Since wholesalers also use the order\n management system, any security defects may result in damage to not only Company M but\n also the wholesalers. Therefore, Company M requested Company S, which provides security\n analytic services, to perform a vulnerability analysis of the order management system.\n\n[Order management system]\n The order management system’s application (hereinafter, the application) runs on a Web\n server. Orders, shipments, and other information are stored in an order information database\n that runs on the database server. The application is used to reference and update the\n information. Files that can be downloaded to or files that were uploaded from wholesaler’s\n PCs are stored on a disk that is connected to the Web server. Figure 1 shows the configuration\n of the order management system.\n\nWholesaler’s internal network (partial)\n\nFW3 The Internet\n\nWhole- Whole-\n … FW1 RPS saler’s PC saler’s PC\n\nFW2\n\nFW: Firewall Web Database Internal … Internal\n RPS: Reverse proxy server server server PC PC\n\nCompany M’s internal network (partial)\n\nFigure 1 Configuration of the order management system\n\nA digital certificate is configured on the RPS. The operator of the wholesaler who utilizes\n the order management system accesses the application via RPS from a browser on the\n wholesaler’s PC, and logs in using a user ID and password on the login screen. At that point,\n the browser on the wholesaler’s PC uses HTTP over TLS (hereinafter, HTTPS) for\n communication, and the RPS uses the digital certificate to convert the protocol from HTTPS\n to HTTP.\n\n[Result of the vulnerability analysis by Company S]\nIn the application, measures have been taken against ***A*** that makes the database\nserver execute unexpected operations to gain unauthorized access to the database. However,\nthe vulnerability analysis by Company S reveals multiple security vulnerabilities that require\nmeasures in the application. Table 1 shows an excerpt of the findings by Company S.\n\nTable 1 Findings by Company S (excerpt)\n\nFinding Cause\nAn operator of a wholesaler can access\nanother wholesaler’s order and shipping\ninformation.\nAn operator of a wholesaler can download (i) An issue exists in the download\narbitrary files on the Web server. processing of files in the application.\nSince there is a ***B*** vulnerability by\nwhich a script can be embedded in a Web\npage by an attacker, there is a possibility\nthat an operator of a wholesaler can be led\nto another Web site that steals the user ID\nand password.\n (ii) When an operator of a wholesaler\n logs in, the user ID is not locked even\n when the operator repeatedly enters\n wrong passwords.\n Note: Shaded parts are not shown.\n\nCompany M took the measures to address the underlined sections (i) and (ii) in Table 1.\nFurthermore, Company M took measures to address the other findings by Company S,\nincluding security education for operators to prevent ***C*** incidents that take\nadvantage of human errors to gain unauthorized access, and decided to launch the order\nmanagement system.\n\nSubquestion 1\n The sections of the communication routes in Figure 1 are numbered 1 through 5 as shown in\n Table 2. From the answer group below, select the correct answer that represents all of the\n communication routes that use HTTPS for communication when the wholesaler’s PC\n accesses the Web server.\n\nTable 2 Communication routes between devices\n\nRoute number Communication route\n 1 Between each wholesaler’s PC and FW3\n 2 Between FW3 and FW1\n 3 Between FW1 and RPS\n 4 Between FW1 and FW2\n 5 Between FW2 and the Web server\n\nAnswer group\n a) 1 b) 1, 2, 3\n c) 1, 2, 3, 4 d) 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\n e) 2, 3, 4 f) 2, 3, 4, 5\n g) 3, 4\n\nSubquestion 2\n From the answer group below, select the appropriate answer to be inserted in each blank\n _______ in the description.\n\nAnswer group for A through C\n a) brute force attacks b) cross-site scripting\n c) dictionary attacks d) directory traversal\n e) DoS attacks f) port scanning\n g) social engineering h) SQL injection\n\nSubquestion 3\n From the answer group below, select the answer that is appropriate as a measure to address\n the underlined section (i) in Table 1.\n\nAnswer group\n a) Making the user specify only the name of the file to be downloaded, and if the file\n exists in the folder set for each wholesaler, performing download processing.\n b) Making the user specify the file to be downloaded by using a relative path, and if the\n file exists, performing download processing.\n c) Making the user specify the file to be downloaded by using an absolute path, and if\n the file exists, performing download processing.\n d) On the browser on the wholesaler’s PC, displaying the structure of all folders and the\n files on the Web server, making the user specify the file to be downloaded, and\n performing download processing.\n\nSubquestion 4\n From the answer group below, select the answer that is appropriate as a security incident that\n can be caused by the vulnerability in the underlined section (ii) in Table 1."}]'::jsonb,
