  '[{"label":"a","text":"A person could view the login operation over the operator’s shoulder, and later log in."},{"label":"b","text":"A person who uses a program that automatically enters possible passwords in\n succession may be able to log in."},{"label":"c","text":"If an operator of a wholesaler loses a memo on which the password is written, a\n person who finds the memo may be able to log in."},{"label":"d","text":"If an operator of a wholesaler uses his or her birthday as a password, a person who\n knows the birthday can log in."}]'::jsonb,
  '[{"subquestion": 1, "blank": null, "answer": "b"}, {"subquestion": 2, "blank": "A", "answer": "h"}, {"subquestion": 2, "blank": "B", "answer": "b"}, {"subquestion": 2, "blank": "C", "answer": "g"}, {"subquestion": 3, "blank": null, "answer": "a"}, {"subquestion": 4, "blank": null, "answer": "b"}]',
  '{"parts":[{"subquestion":1,"blank":null,"answer":"b"},{"subquestion":2,"blank":"A","answer":"h"},{"subquestion":2,"blank":"B","answer":"b"},{"subquestion":2,"blank":"C","answer":"g"},{"subquestion":3,"blank":null,"answer":"a"},{"subquestion":4,"blank":null,"answer":"b"}]}'::jsonb,
  'Subquestion 1: the browser uses HTTPS only as far as the reverse proxy, which holds the certificate and converts to HTTP, so the encrypted routes are 1, 2, 3 (PC to FW3, FW3 to FW1, FW1 to RPS); from the RPS onward through FW2 to the Web server (routes 4 and 5) the traffic is plain HTTP. Blank A is SQL injection, the attack that makes the database server run unexpected SQL; blank B is cross-site scripting, where an attacker embeds a script in a Web page; blank C is social engineering, which exploits human error rather than technical flaws. Subquestion 3: the download flaw (i) is a directory traversal risk, so the safe fix is to let the user give only a file name and serve it only if it exists in that wholesaler''s own folder; relative or absolute paths and showing the folder tree all let users reach other files. Subquestion 4: because the ID is never locked after repeated wrong passwords, a program that automatically tries passwords one after another (a brute force attack) can eventually log in; the other choices are not prevented by a lockout.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'security-threats'),
