  'SQL1 subtracts the quantities still to be delivered from stock, so blank A is P.Quantity and blank B is SUM(D.Quantity), giving for example LC100: 800 − 10 = 790 and MP080: 20 − 4 = 16. Blank C must be LEFT OUTER JOIN so that products with no pending deliveries (LC050, MP032) still appear; their SUM is NULL, which the CASE turns into 0 so the stock is shown unchanged. An inner join would drop those two rows. In SQL2, blank D is UPDATE Employee because the Salary column belongs to the Employee table, blank E is O.OrderID = D.OrderID to join each order with its detail lines, and blank F is GROUP BY O.EmployeeID HAVING SUM(D.Amount) > 10000, since a condition on an aggregate must go in HAVING, not WHERE. Frank Fairchild (E003) handled order 102102 worth 3000 + 8000 = 11000, so only he qualifies.',
