  'Q4. Read the following description of a network in an institute, and then answer Subquestions
 1 and 2.

A training institute V has a computer lab named Computer Lab 1. It has 8 PCs for trainees,
 a PC for a trainer, a file server, a printer, a 16-port manageable 10/100 switch, and a
 10/100/1000 router.
 The institute has several public IP addresses that allow LAN users to access the Internet. A
 network administrator uses a private IP address 192.168.1.0/28 for configuring PCs, printers,
 and servers in Computer lab 1. The institute subscribes to SaaS for efficient training. The
 SaaS provides contents such as instructional videos for trainers, textbooks, material files for
 trainees, and online applications. Generally, there is no major issue with the total network
 system.
 Figure 1 shows the institute network and Table 1 shows some of the IP addresses.

Figure 1 The institute network

Table 1 Some of the IP addresses

Location IP address (range) Comment
 Computer Lab 1 192.168.1.0/28 Private network (Used for local communication)
 Central Router 198.51.100.20 Used for NAPT
 SaaS Web Server1 203.0.113.55 A public Web server on the Internet

The central router uses public IP address 198.51.100.20 to enable LAN users to browse
materials on the Internet. The network administrator configured Network Address Port
Translation (NAPT), also known as NAT overload, in the central router. The NAPT enables
inside local IP addresses (private addresses on the LAN) to hide from the Internet and allows
local users to access the Internet. When PCs on the LAN send requests/packets to SaaS Web
server1, the packets pass through the central router. The central router replaces the inside
local IP addresses of the packets with the public IP address of the router. It also attaches
individual port numbers with each IP address to map the inside local IP address of the sender
PC with the public IP address of the router. The router receives a response from the Internet
along with the same port number it attached when sending the request/packet. Using this
port number, the router translates the public IP address back to the inside local IP address
and delivers the responded message to the right destination on the LAN.
Figure 2 shows the connection diagram and communication path. Table 2 shows port
numbers and IP addresses at a different stage of communication when the packet goes from
PCs to SaaS Web Server1.

Inside local (private LAN) The Internet
 SaaS Web Server1
 Central the Internet
 Router

198.51.100.20 203.0.113.55 IP address range: 192.168.1.1 to
 192.168.1.15

Figure 2 Connection diagram and communication path

Table 2 Port numbers and IP addresses at a different stage of communication

Inside local (address:port) The Internet (address:port)
 Source Destination Source Destination
 192.168.1.5:3312 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:5589 203.0.113.55:80
 192.168.1.6:4524 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:3312 203.0.113.55:80
 192.168.1.7:8923 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:8923 203.0.113.55:80
 192.168.1.8:12870 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:8924 203.0.113.55:80
 192.168.1.7:3312 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:3313 203.0.113.55:80

When SaaS Web Server1 responds to a request of a PC on the LAN, the translation process
goes in reverse order. For example, when SaaS Web Server1 wants to send a response to one
of a request in Table-2, it will create a packet with source and destination address along with
port number as follows:

Source address:port Destination address:port
 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:3312

In this case, the packet will finally be received by the PC whose IP address is ***A*** .

[Establishment of Computer Lab 2]
Training institute V has established Computer Lab 2 for a larger class. The network
configuration of Computer Lab 2 will be the same as Computer Lab 1, except that there will
be 20 PCs for trainees. Figure 3 shows the new institute network.

Figure 3 New institute network

The network administrator assigns the next assignable address block so that the new subnet
 accommodates the minimum number of IP addresses that can cover all network devices in
 Computer Lab 2.

The network administrator assigns the IP addresses within the IP address block to the PCs,
 printer, server, and router in Computer Lab 2.
 The new subnet mask is ***B*** , and the IP address range is ***C*** . The network
 administrator uses the last assignable IP address of the second subnet as the default gateway
 address of that network. For Computer Lab 2, ***D*** is used as the default gateway.

Subquestion 1
 From the answer groups below, select the correct answer to be inserted in each blank
 _______ in the above description.

Answer group for A
 a) 192.168.1.5 b) 192.168.1.6
 c) 192.168.1.7 d) 192.168.1.8

Answer group for B
 a) 255.255.255.0 b) 255.255.255.128
 c) 255.255.255.192 d) 255.255.255.224
 e) 255.255.255.240

Answer group for C
 a) 192.168.1.16 - 31/28 b) 192.168.1.16 - 47/27
 c) 192.168.1.32 - 63/27 d) 192.168.1.64 - 127/26
 e) 192.168.1.128 - 255/25

Answer group for D
 a) 192.168.1.30 b) 192.168.1.31
 c) 192.168.1.62 d) 192.168.1.63
 e) 192.168.1.126 f) 192.168.1.127

Subquestion 2
 From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted in each blank
 _______ in the following description.

The contents and online applications provided by SaaS have recently been renewed, and the
current Internet connection 1Gbps does not have enough bandwidth to use the new contents.
Therefore, the decision was made to upgrade the Internet connection and replace the
switches and routers.

Table 3 shows the interface specifications of the devices before and after replacement.

Table 3 Interface specifications of the devices

Device Before replacement After replacement
 Central Router 1Gbps: 4 ports 10Gbps: 4 ports
 Central Switch 1Gbps: 2 ports 10Gbps: 16 ports
 100Mbps: 16 ports
 Router 1, Router 2 1Gbps: 2 ports 10Gbps: 2 ports
 Switch 1, Switch 2 1Gbps: 2 ports 10Gbps: 2 ports
 100Mbps: 24 ports 1Gbps: 24 ports
 PCs for trainees and trainer 100Mbps: 1 port 1Gbps: 1 port
 File server 1, File server 2 1Gbps: 1 port 1Gbps: 1 port
 Printer1, Printer 2 100Mbps: 1 port 100Mbps: 1 port

After replacement, all the hosts are connected to the 1Gbps ports of the switches, and the
switches are connected to the routers via a 10Gbps port. All the devices are interconnected
by UTP cat5 cables. No cables are shorter than 3 meters and longer than 20 meters.
There are some training classes where the trainers conduct classes on 3D modeling, video
editing, and rendering using online applications or tools provided by the SaaS. If the training
class wants to work with those online applications in real time, they need at least 1Gbps even
in some cases 3 to 4Gbps bandwidth between the classroom and the SaaS server farm. The
performance of the PCs is well capable of handling all the tasks. However, when the entire
class wants to follow the trainer using the online applications, the bandwidth significantly
drops and becomes insufficient for trainees to work smoothly. The network administrator
checks all the devices, connections, and configurations, and suggests to ***E*** to
increase the bandwidth per computer.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q4. Read the following description of a network in an institute, and then answer Subquestions\n 1 and 2."},{"type":"context","text":"A training institute V has a computer lab named Computer Lab 1. It has 8 PCs for trainees,\n a PC for a trainer, a file server, a printer, a 16-port manageable 10/100 switch, and a\n 10/100/1000 router.\n The institute has several public IP addresses that allow LAN users to access the Internet. A\n network administrator uses a private IP address 192.168.1.0/28 for configuring PCs, printers,\n and servers in Computer lab 1. The institute subscribes to SaaS for efficient training. The\n SaaS provides contents such as instructional videos for trainers, textbooks, material files for\n trainees, and online applications. Generally, there is no major issue with the total network\n system.\n Figure 1 shows the institute network and Table 1 shows some of the IP addresses.\n\nFigure 1 The institute network\n\nTable 1 Some of the IP addresses\n\nLocation IP address (range) Comment\n Computer Lab 1 192.168.1.0/28 Private network (Used for local communication)\n Central Router 198.51.100.20 Used for NAPT\n SaaS Web Server1 203.0.113.55 A public Web server on the Internet\n\nThe central router uses public IP address 198.51.100.20 to enable LAN users to browse\nmaterials on the Internet. The network administrator configured Network Address Port\nTranslation (NAPT), also known as NAT overload, in the central router. The NAPT enables\ninside local IP addresses (private addresses on the LAN) to hide from the Internet and allows\nlocal users to access the Internet. When PCs on the LAN send requests/packets to SaaS Web\nserver1, the packets pass through the central router. The central router replaces the inside\nlocal IP addresses of the packets with the public IP address of the router. It also attaches\nindividual port numbers with each IP address to map the inside local IP address of the sender\nPC with the public IP address of the router. The router receives a response from the Internet\nalong with the same port number it attached when sending the request/packet. Using this\nport number, the router translates the public IP address back to the inside local IP address\nand delivers the responded message to the right destination on the LAN.\nFigure 2 shows the connection diagram and communication path. Table 2 shows port\nnumbers and IP addresses at a different stage of communication when the packet goes from\nPCs to SaaS Web Server1.\n\nInside local (private LAN) The Internet\n SaaS Web Server1\n Central the Internet\n Router\n\n198.51.100.20 203.0.113.55 IP address range: 192.168.1.1 to\n 192.168.1.15\n\nFigure 2 Connection diagram and communication path\n\nTable 2 Port numbers and IP addresses at a different stage of communication\n\nInside local (address:port) The Internet (address:port)\n Source Destination Source Destination\n 192.168.1.5:3312 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:5589 203.0.113.55:80\n 192.168.1.6:4524 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:3312 203.0.113.55:80\n 192.168.1.7:8923 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:8923 203.0.113.55:80\n 192.168.1.8:12870 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:8924 203.0.113.55:80\n 192.168.1.7:3312 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:3313 203.0.113.55:80\n\nWhen SaaS Web Server1 responds to a request of a PC on the LAN, the translation process\ngoes in reverse order. For example, when SaaS Web Server1 wants to send a response to one\nof a request in Table-2, it will create a packet with source and destination address along with\nport number as follows:\n\nSource address:port Destination address:port\n 203.0.113.55:80 198.51.100.20:3312\n\nIn this case, the packet will finally be received by the PC whose IP address is ***A*** .\n\n[Establishment of Computer Lab 2]\nTraining institute V has established Computer Lab 2 for a larger class. The network\nconfiguration of Computer Lab 2 will be the same as Computer Lab 1, except that there will\nbe 20 PCs for trainees. Figure 3 shows the new institute network.\n\nFigure 3 New institute network\n\nThe network administrator assigns the next assignable address block so that the new subnet\n accommodates the minimum number of IP addresses that can cover all network devices in\n Computer Lab 2.\n\nThe network administrator assigns the IP addresses within the IP address block to the PCs,\n printer, server, and router in Computer Lab 2.\n The new subnet mask is ***B*** , and the IP address range is ***C*** . The network\n administrator uses the last assignable IP address of the second subnet as the default gateway\n address of that network. For Computer Lab 2, ***D*** is used as the default gateway.\n\nSubquestion 1\n From the answer groups below, select the correct answer to be inserted in each blank\n _______ in the above description.\n\nAnswer group for A\n a) 192.168.1.5 b) 192.168.1.6\n c) 192.168.1.7 d) 192.168.1.8\n\nAnswer group for B\n a) 255.255.255.0 b) 255.255.255.128\n c) 255.255.255.192 d) 255.255.255.224\n e) 255.255.255.240\n\nAnswer group for C\n a) 192.168.1.16 - 31/28 b) 192.168.1.16 - 47/27\n c) 192.168.1.32 - 63/27 d) 192.168.1.64 - 127/26\n e) 192.168.1.128 - 255/25\n\nAnswer group for D\n a) 192.168.1.30 b) 192.168.1.31\n c) 192.168.1.62 d) 192.168.1.63\n e) 192.168.1.126 f) 192.168.1.127\n\nSubquestion 2\n From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted in each blank\n _______ in the following description.\n\nThe contents and online applications provided by SaaS have recently been renewed, and the\ncurrent Internet connection 1Gbps does not have enough bandwidth to use the new contents.\nTherefore, the decision was made to upgrade the Internet connection and replace the\nswitches and routers.\n\nTable 3 shows the interface specifications of the devices before and after replacement.\n\nTable 3 Interface specifications of the devices\n\nDevice Before replacement After replacement\n Central Router 1Gbps: 4 ports 10Gbps: 4 ports\n Central Switch 1Gbps: 2 ports 10Gbps: 16 ports\n 100Mbps: 16 ports\n Router 1, Router 2 1Gbps: 2 ports 10Gbps: 2 ports\n Switch 1, Switch 2 1Gbps: 2 ports 10Gbps: 2 ports\n 100Mbps: 24 ports 1Gbps: 24 ports\n PCs for trainees and trainer 100Mbps: 1 port 1Gbps: 1 port\n File server 1, File server 2 1Gbps: 1 port 1Gbps: 1 port\n Printer1, Printer 2 100Mbps: 1 port 100Mbps: 1 port\n\nAfter replacement, all the hosts are connected to the 1Gbps ports of the switches, and the\nswitches are connected to the routers via a 10Gbps port. All the devices are interconnected\nby UTP cat5 cables. No cables are shorter than 3 meters and longer than 20 meters.\nThere are some training classes where the trainers conduct classes on 3D modeling, video\nediting, and rendering using online applications or tools provided by the SaaS. If the training\nclass wants to work with those online applications in real time, they need at least 1Gbps even\nin some cases 3 to 4Gbps bandwidth between the classroom and the SaaS server farm. The\nperformance of the PCs is well capable of handling all the tasks. However, when the entire\nclass wants to follow the trainer using the online applications, the bandwidth significantly\ndrops and becomes insufficient for trainees to work smoothly. The network administrator\nchecks all the devices, connections, and configurations, and suggests to ***E*** to\nincrease the bandwidth per computer."}]'::jsonb,
  '["2022-october-b-q04-figure1.webp","2022-october-b-q04-figure2.webp","2022-october-b-q04-figure3.webp","2022-october-b-q04-figure4.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"replace all the CAT-5 cables with CAT-6a cables"},{"label":"b","text":"replace all the crossover cables with straight-through cables"},{"label":"c","text":"replace all the PCs with a PC that has higher memory and processing power"},{"label":"d","text":"replace the switch with a hub to create a large collision domain"}]'::jsonb,
  '[{"subquestion": 1, "blank": "A", "answer": "b"}, {"subquestion": 1, "blank": "B", "answer": "d"}, {"subquestion": 1, "blank": "C", "answer": "c"}, {"subquestion": 1, "blank": "D", "answer": "c"}, {"subquestion": 2, "blank": "E", "answer": "a"}]',
  '{"parts":[{"subquestion":1,"blank":"A","answer":"b"},{"subquestion":1,"blank":"B","answer":"d"},{"subquestion":1,"blank":"C","answer":"c"},{"subquestion":1,"blank":"D","answer":"c"},{"subquestion":2,"blank":"E","answer":"a"}]}'::jsonb,
  'Blank A: in the NAPT table, public port 3312 on 198.51.100.20 maps back to inside address 192.168.1.6:4524, so the response reaches 192.168.1.6 (port 3312 on 192.168.1.5 is only the inside port and is irrelevant). Lab 2 needs addresses for 20 trainee PCs, the trainer PC, a file server, a printer and the router port, 24 hosts, which with the network and broadcast addresses needs a 32-address block. So blank B is the /27 mask 255.255.255.224, and blank C is the next /27-aligned block after 192.168.1.0/28, which is 192.168.1.32 - 63/27 (192.168.1.16 is not a valid /27 boundary). Blank D, the last assignable address of that block, is 192.168.1.62 because .63 is the broadcast address. Blank E: the hosts and links are now 1 Gbps and 10 Gbps but CAT-5 cable is only rated for 100 Mbps, so replacing it with CAT-6a (10 Gbps) raises the bandwidth; the PCs are already powerful enough and a hub would make things worse.',
