  'Step 1 counts occurrences, so blank A is Count[Score[i]] (incremented by 1). Step 2 gives each value present its starting position: blank B sets Index[i] to the running total n, and blank C adds Count[i] to n, producing Index[7] = 3, Index[8] = 5 and so on. Step 3 takes the slot for each score with blank D, n ← Index[Score[i]], then blank E advances that slot with Index[Score[i]] ← Index[Score[i]] + 1 so the next equal score goes to the following position. Every loop runs a fixed number of times over the data (plus the small value range), so the cost is O(n) (blank F). For descending order, method 2 mirrors each position with n ← 9 − n (blank G); this reverses the order of equal scores, so it is unstable, whereas method 1 just assigns starting positions from the highest value down and still copies in input order, so it is stable (blank H).',
