  'Q7. Read the following description of a C program, and then answer Subquestions 1 and 2.

 Heap is a data structure using a complete binary tree. The complete binary tree is a tree
 satisfying the following conditions:
 o Each node in the tree may have at most two child nodes.
 o The number of nodes at depth N is 2N except at the bottom of the tree.
 o The nodes at the bottom of the tree are filled from left to right.

 Heap satisfies the following condition in addition to the above:
 o Any parent node in a complete binary tree has a greater value than or equal to both of its
 child nodes have.

 Figure 1 shows an example of a heap.

 Depth 0 15

 Depth 1 10 8

 Depth 2 5 9 3

 Figure 1 Example of a heap

 A heap can be built onto an array by regarding i-th element of the array as the parent node
 of the (2×i + 1)-th and (2×i + 2)-th elements. Figure 2 shows how the heap in Figure 1 is
 built onto an array.

 0 1 2 3 4 5
 15 10 8 5 9 3

 Figure 2 How the heap in Figure 1 is built onto an array

 Heap sort is a sorting technique based on the heap. The following steps show how elements
 in an array are sorted by heap sort.

(1) For each element of a given array, apply (i) through (iii) to create the heap.
 (i) Include an element to the heap at the rightmost node at the bottom of the heap.
 (ii) Check if the element has a greater value than its parent has. If it does, swap them.
 (iii) Fix the heap by recursively applying (ii) upward while swapping occurs and the
 element is not the first of the array.
(2) While the number of nodes in a heap is greater than 1, apply (i) through (iv).
 (i) Swap the root node with the rightmost node at the bottom of the heap.
 (ii) Exclude the rightmost node at the bottom of the heap from the heap.
 (iii) Check if the parent node has less value than either of the child nodes has. If it does,
 swap the root node with the child node with the greatest value.
 (iv) Fix the heap by recursively applying (iii) downward while swapping occurs and the
 node has a child node.

As a result of the steps above, the elements are sorted in ascending order within the array.
Figure 3 shows how the heap sort is applied to the heap in Figure 2.

 swap

 15 10 8 5 9 3 3 10 8 5 9 15

 heap heap (fix needed) sorted

 swap

 10 9 8 5 3 15 3 9 8 5 10 15

 heap (fixed) heap (fix needed) sorted

 swap

 9 5 8 3 10 15 3 5 8 9 10 15

 heap (fixed) heap (fix needed) sorted

 swap

 8 5 3 9 10 15 3 5 8 9 10 15

 heap (fixed) heap (fix needed) sorted

 swap

 5 3 8 9 10 15 3 5 8 9 10 15

 heap (fixed) sorted (finish)

 Figure 3 How heap sort is applied to the heap in Figure 2

[Program Description]
This program sorts students by their exam score using the heap sort. Each student is assigned
an ID, a serial number starting at 0.

The program uses the user-defined data type, user-defined functions, and the compile-time
operator.

User-defined data type:
 Name Description
 Data_t A user-defined data type represents the ID and value of the data.

User-defined functions:
 Name Description
 void swap(Data_t *a, Data_t *b) Swaps the values pointed by a and b.
 void heap_up(Data_t *data, Recursively fixes the heap. The processing
 int child) starts from the child and proceeds upward.
 Void heap_down(Data_t *data, Recursively fixes the heap. The processing
 int heap_size, starts from the parent and proceeds
 int parent) downward.
 void rank_by_score(Data_t *data, Sorts n elements of data in ascending order
 int n) using functions heap_up and heap_down.
 void print_data(Data_t *data, Prints n elements of data.

 int n)

Compile-time operator:
 Name Description
