  'A',
  25,
  'Q25. Which of the following is an appropriate explanation of a relational database?',
  '[{"type":"text","page":10,"bbox":[65.22,643.74,483.74,675.19],"text":"Q25.\nWhich of the following is an appropriate explanation of a relational database?"},{"type":"text","page":10,"bbox":[84.12,680.93,439.4,766.21],"text":"a) It connects a parent record and a child record by using a pointer.\nb) It represents data in the form of a two-dimensional table.\nc) It represents the structure and meaning of data by using tags.\nd) It retains data and procedures in an integrated (or encapsulated) form."},{"type":"code","page":10,"bbox":[65.22,794.63,314.58,807.97],"text":"– 10 –"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"It connects a parent record and a child record by using a pointer."},{"label":"b","text":"It represents data in the form of a two-dimensional table."},{"label":"c","text":"It represents the structure and meaning of data by using tags."},{"label":"d","text":"It retains data and procedures in an integrated (or encapsulated) form."}]'::jsonb,
  'b',
  '{"value":"b"}'::jsonb,
  'A relational database represents data as two-dimensional tables made of rows and columns. Linking parent and child records with pointers describes hierarchical or network databases, tags describe XML, and encapsulating data with procedures describes an object-oriented database.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'databases'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2023S, FE, Subject-A, Q25)',
  ARRAY[10]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q25. Which of the following is an appropriate explanation of a relational database?

 a) It connects a parent record and a child record by using a pointer.
 b) It represents data in the form of a two-dimensional table.
 c) It represents the structure and meaning of data by using tags.
 d) It retains data and procedures in an integrated (or encapsulated) form.',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[10]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2023-april',
  'A',
  26,
  'Q26. Which of the following is the key of a relation schema R = (M, N, O, P, S, T), when R
 has the functional dependencies shown below?

 O → T
 S → M
 OS → P
 M → N',
  '[{"type":"text","page":11,"bbox":[65.22,68.76,533.1,118.21],"text":"Q26.\nWhich of the following is the key of a relation schema R = (M, N, O, P, S, T), when R\nhas the functional dependencies shown below?"},{"type":"text","page":11,"bbox":[65.22,122.93,216.48,208.21],"text":"O → T\nS → M\nOS → P\nM → N"},{"type":"text","page":11,"bbox":[65.22,212.93,472.74,244.21],"text":"a) MO\nb) MS\nc) OP\nd) OS"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"MO"},{"label":"b","text":"MS"},{"label":"c","text":"OP"},{"label":"d","text":"OS"}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'The key is OS. From O we get T; from S we get M and then N via M → N; and OS → P gives P, so the closure of {O, S} is all six attributes. Neither O nor S alone determines everything, and no other attribute determines O or S, so both must be in the key.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'databases'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2023S, FE, Subject-A, Q26)',
  ARRAY[11]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q26. Which of the following is the key of a relation schema R = (M, N, O, P, S, T), when R
 has the functional dependencies shown below?

 O → T
 S → M
 OS → P
 M → N

 a) MO b) MS c) OP d) OS',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[11]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2023-april',
  'A',
  27,
  'Q27. Which of the following is an appropriate description of keys in a relational database?',
  '[{"type":"text","page":11,"bbox":[65.22,248.93,523.98,283.42],"text":"Q27.\nWhich of the following is an appropriate description of keys in a relational database?"},{"type":"text","page":11,"bbox":[84.12,302.93,511.74,316.21],"text":"a) A primary key can be assigned as a composite key and must contain a column that is"},{"type":"text","page":11,"bbox":[84.12,320.93,515.37,370.21],"text":"defined as a foreign key.\nb) The values of the primary and foreign keys cannot be duplicated and cannot be null.\nc) The values of the primary key must be unique, and the values of the foreign key must"},{"type":"text","page":11,"bbox":[84.12,374.93,513.4,406.21],"text":"match the values of the related table’s primary key and can be duplicated.\nd) The values of the primary key need to match the values of the related table’s primary"},{"type":"text","page":11,"bbox":[65.22,410.93,505.02,477.13],"text":"key, but the values of the foreign key do not need to match the values of the related\ntable’s primary key."}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"A primary key can be assigned as a composite key and must contain a column that is\n defined as a foreign key."},{"label":"b","text":"The values of the primary and foreign keys cannot be duplicated and cannot be null."},{"label":"c","text":"The values of the primary key must be unique, and the values of the foreign key must\n match the values of the related table’s primary key and can be duplicated."},{"label":"d","text":"The values of the primary key need to match the values of the related table’s primary\n key, but the values of the foreign key do not need to match the values of the related\n table’s primary key."}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'Primary key values must be unique (and non-null), while foreign key values must match a primary key value in the referenced table and may be duplicated. Many rows can refer to the same parent row, so a foreign key column often contains repeated values, which is why the choice saying foreign keys cannot be duplicated is wrong.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'databases'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2023S, FE, Subject-A, Q27)',
  ARRAY[11]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q27. Which of the following is an appropriate description of keys in a relational database?

 a) A primary key can be assigned as a composite key and must contain a column that is
 defined as a foreign key.
 b) The values of the primary and foreign keys cannot be duplicated and cannot be null.
 c) The values of the primary key must be unique, and the values of the foreign key must
 match the values of the related table’s primary key and can be duplicated.
 d) The values of the primary key need to match the values of the related table’s primary
 key, but the values of the foreign key do not need to match the values of the related
 table’s primary key.',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[11]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2023-april',
  'A',
  28,
  'Q28. Which of the following is the appropriate interpretation of the conceptual data model shown in the diagram in UML?',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q28. Which of the following is the appropriate interpretation of the conceptual data model shown in the diagram in UML?"}]'::jsonb,
  '["2023-april-a-q28-figure1.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"A department cannot exist without any employees."},{"label":"b","text":"An employee can belong to multiple departments at the same time."},{"label":"c","text":"An employee does not have to belong to a department."},{"label":"d","text":"The total number of employees and the total number of departments match."}]'::jsonb,
  'b',
  '{"value":"b"}'::jsonb,
  'The diagram means an employee can belong to multiple departments at the same time. The multiplicity next to Department is 1..*, so each employee belongs to at least one and possibly many departments, and the 0..* next to Employee means a department may have no employees. This also rules out the choices that a department needs employees and that an employee need not belong to a department.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'databases'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2023S, FE, Subject-A, Q28)',
  ARRAY[11]::int[],
  'manual_review',
  '3.0',
  'Q28. Which of the following is the appropriate interpretation of the conceptual data model
 shown in the diagram in UML?

 Belongs to
 Department Employee
 1..* 0..*

 a) A department cannot exist without any employees.
 b) An employee can belong to multiple departments at the same time.
 c) An employee does not have to belong to a department.
 d) The total number of employees and the total number of departments match.',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[11]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2023-april',
  'A',
  29,
  'Q29. An employees table contains fields in the order shown below.

 • emp_id is an integer value and is used as the primary key of the table.
 • emp_name can store up to 10 characters.
 • salary is a decimal value up to 4 digits.

 Which of the following is an appropriate INSERT statement to insert the data to the table? Here,
 NOT NULL constraint does not exist and the table does not have any records before the insert.',
  '[{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[65.22,67.74,405.42,81.19],"text":"Q29.\nAn employees table contains fields in the order shown below."},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[83.22,104.7,439.14,118.15],"text":"• emp_id is an integer value and is used as the primary key of the table."},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[83.22,123.9,299.73,137.35],"text":"• emp_name can store up to 10 characters."},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[83.22,143.1,291.15,156.55],"text":"• salary is a decimal value up to 4 digits."},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[76.8,180.65,533.2,193.93],"text":"Which of the following is an appropriate INSERT statement to insert the data to the table? Here,"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[65.22,198.65,533.1,229.93],"text":"NOT NULL constraint does not exist and the table does not have any records before the insert."},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[79.5,234.65,527.07,265.93],"text":"a)\nINSERT INTO employees (emp_id, emp_name) VALUES (4, ''Hulk'');\nb)\nINSERT INTO employees (emp_id, salary, emp_name) VALUES (3, ''Taylor'',"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[105.0,272.05,147.25,282.07],"text":"1700);"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[79.5,288.65,502.93,301.93],"text":"c)\nINSERT INTO employees (emp_id, salary, emp_name) VALUES (5, 1900,"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[105.0,308.05,201.48,318.07],"text":"''Bartholomew'');"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[79.5,324.65,406.49,337.93],"text":"d)\nINSERT INTO employees VALUES (2, ''Harry'', 15000);"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, emp_name) VALUES (4, ''Hulk'');"},{"label":"b","text":"INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, salary, emp_name) VALUES (3, ''Taylor'',\n\n 1700);"},{"label":"c","text":"INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, salary, emp_name) VALUES (5, 1900,\n\n ''Bartholomew'');"},{"label":"d","text":"INSERT INTO employees VALUES (2, ''Harry'', 15000);"}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'The valid statement inserts only emp_id 4 and emp_name ''Hulk''; salary is left as NULL, which is allowed because there is no NOT NULL constraint. The other statements fail: one puts ''Taylor'' into the numeric salary column because the value order does not match the column list, ''Bartholomew'' has 11 characters and exceeds the 10-character limit, and 15000 has five digits, more than the 4 digits salary allows.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'databases'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2023S, FE, Subject-A, Q29)',
  ARRAY[12]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q29. An employees table contains fields in the order shown below.

 • emp_id is an integer value and is used as the primary key of the table.
 • emp_name can store up to 10 characters.
 • salary is a decimal value up to 4 digits.

 Which of the following is an appropriate INSERT statement to insert the data to the table? Here,
 NOT NULL constraint does not exist and the table does not have any records before the insert.

 a) INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, emp_name) VALUES (4, ''Hulk'');
 b) INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, salary, emp_name) VALUES (3, ''Taylor'',

 1700);
 c) INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, salary, emp_name) VALUES (5, 1900,

 ''Bartholomew'');
 d) INSERT INTO employees VALUES (2, ''Harry'', 15000);',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[12]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2023-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
