  'Blank A is the CA''s private key: the CA signs the server certificate with its private key, which is why anyone can verify the signature with the CA''s public key held in the CA certificate. Blank B is (2) ServerHello, because that is the message in which the server picks the cipher suite and other parameters, completing the agreement on connection parameters. Blank C is the session key, since the agreed master secret is what both sides use to derive the symmetric session key. Blank D is (6) ClientKeyExchange: only after receiving the client''s key-exchange contribution does the server have everything needed to compute the master secret. Blank E is the server''s public key, because in RSA key exchange the client encrypts the premaster secret with the public key from the server certificate so only the server can decrypt it. Blank F is the server''s private key: if it leaks, every recorded RSA-exchanged premaster secret can be decrypted, whereas DHE-RSA uses RSA only for signing, so a leaked private key does not expose past sessions (forward secrecy).',
