  'Blank A: the weakness of linked allocation is that it is poor for direct (random) access, because reaching a given part of the file means following the chain of blocks from the beginning. Blank B: indexed allocation needs a whole index block per file, so it wastes more space than linked allocation when there are many small files. Blank C is 11 and 11: with linked allocation each 4 kB block loses 4 bytes to the next-block number, so 40 kB (40,960 bytes) no longer fits in 10 blocks of 4,092 data bytes and needs 11, while indexed allocation needs 10 data blocks plus 1 index block. Blank D is 257: 1 MB is 256 data blocks; 12 are reached by direct pointers and the remaining 244 through the single indirect pointer, which costs one extra pointer block. Blank E is about 16 GB: with 1 kB blocks and 4-byte pointers a block holds 256 pointers, so the triple indirect level reaches 256 × 256 × 256 blocks × 1 kB = 2²⁴ kB = 16 GB, which dominates the total.',
