  'In SQL1, blank A is SUM(Amount) to total the payments per store, blank B is Payment p because the later join condition refers to the alias p, and blank C is Sales DESC to list stores from highest to lowest sales (store 20 gets 2.99 + 9.99 = 12.98 through staff S002). In SQL2, blank D is (FirstName LIKE ''B%'') AND (LastName LIKE ''Gre_n''): the first name only has to start with B (any length), while the last name has exactly five characters, so one underscore stands for the single unknown fourth character; ''Gre%n'' would also match longer names and ''B_'' would demand a two-letter first name. In SQL3, blank E puts the date range in WHERE (BETWEEN ''2023-04-01'' AND ''2023-04-15''), groups by customer, and filters the groups with HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2; an aggregate like COUNT(*) cannot appear in WHERE, and grouping by PayDate would split each customer''s payments into separate groups. Only C003 has two payments in that period.',
