  'A',
  47,
  'Q47. Which of the following is the most appropriate feature of polymorphism in object-oriented programming?',
  '[{"type":"text","page":15,"bbox":[65.22,68.76,503.15,100.21],"text":"Q47.\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate feature of polymorphism in object-\noriented programming?"},{"type":"text","page":15,"bbox":[84.12,122.93,497.72,136.21],"text":"a) Developers are allowed to define classes incrementally by reusing classes defined"},{"type":"text","page":15,"bbox":[84.12,140.93,527.94,208.21],"text":"previously as the basis for new classes.\nb) Only the information required to use a software module is published to the module user.\nc) The processes and data are combined into a single object.\nd) The same message can be interpreted differently by different classes of objects."}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"Developers are allowed to define classes incrementally by reusing classes defined\n previously as the basis for new classes."},{"label":"b","text":"Only the information required to use a software module is published to the module user."},{"label":"c","text":"The processes and data are combined into a single object."},{"label":"d","text":"The same message can be interpreted differently by different classes of objects."}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'Polymorphism means the same message (method call) can be interpreted differently by objects of different classes, each responding with its own implementation. Reusing existing classes to define new ones describes inheritance, publishing only what users need describes information hiding, and combining processes and data into one object describes encapsulation.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'software-development'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2023A, FE, Subject-A, Q47)',
