(
  '2023-october',
  'A',
  51,
  'Q51. The ten members of a project team hold meetings with each other with the conditions
 below. If two (2) members are to be added to the team, how many additional meetings will
 be held compared to if there were ten members?

 [Conditions]
 • Meetings are held one-on-one.
 • Each member holds one meeting with every other member.',
  '[{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[65.22,68.76,533.19,136.21],"text":"Q51.\nThe ten members of a project team hold meetings with each other with the conditions\nbelow. If two (2) members are to be added to the team, how many additional meetings will\nbe held compared to if there were ten members?"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[100.68,140.93,164.34,154.21],"text":"[Conditions]"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[84.12,158.93,470.1,226.21],"text":"•\nMeetings are held one-on-one.\n•\nEach member holds one meeting with every other member.\n\na) 12\nb) 21\nc) 22\nd) 42"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"12"},{"label":"b","text":"21"},{"label":"c","text":"22"},{"label":"d","text":"42"}]'::jsonb,
  'b',
  '{"value":"b"}'::jsonb,
  'There will be 21 additional meetings. With one-on-one meetings between every pair, n members hold n(n − 1)/2 meetings: 12 members give 12 × 11 / 2 = 66 and 10 members give 10 × 9 / 2 = 45, so the increase is 66 − 45 = 21. The same formula is used to count communication channels in a project team.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'project-management'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2023A, FE, Subject-A, Q51)',
  ARRAY[16]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q51. The ten members of a project team hold meetings with each other with the conditions
 below. If two (2) members are to be added to the team, how many additional meetings will
 be held compared to if there were ten members?

 [Conditions]
 • Meetings are held one-on-one.
 • Each member holds one meeting with every other member.

 a) 12 b) 21 c) 22 d) 42',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2023-october/a/questions.pdf","pages":[16]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2023-october/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2023-october',
  'A',
  52,
  'Q52. In order to determine the quality condition in a test process, it is necessary to analyze
 and evaluate the relationship between the number of completed test items and the
 cumulative number of bugs. Which of the following is a graph that indicates that the quality
 is becoming stable?',
  '[{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[65.22,266.76,530.16,352.21],"text":"Q52.\nIn order to determine the quality condition in a test process, it is necessary to analyze\nand evaluate the relationship between the number of completed test items and the\ncumulative number of bugs. Which of the following is a graph that indicates that the quality\nis becoming stable?"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[102.36,358.55,114.66,371.83],"text":"a)"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[251.58,358.55,319.26,371.83],"text":"b)"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[135.79,365.97,149.27,442.38],"text":"number of bugs"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[341.35,365.97,354.83,442.38],"text":"number of bugs"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[123.91,372.76,137.39,432.54],"text":"Cumulative"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[329.47,372.76,342.95,432.54],"text":"Cumulative"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[145.8,447.01,243.7,472.67],"text":"Number of\ncompleted test items"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[351.36,447.73,449.19,473.39],"text":"Number of\ncompleted test items"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[102.36,511.55,114.66,524.83],"text":"c)"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[251.58,511.55,319.26,524.83],"text":"d)"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[341.31,516.89,354.99,594.3],"text":"number of bugs"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[135.79,518.16,149.27,594.54],"text":"number of bugs"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[329.31,523.7,342.99,584.28],"text":"Cumulative"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[123.91,525.0,137.39,584.82],"text":"Cumulative"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[145.8,600.01,243.7,625.67],"text":"Number of\ncompleted test items"},{"type":"text","page":16,"bbox":[351.48,601.41,450.09,627.45],"text":"Number of\ncompleted test items"},{"type":"code","page":16,"bbox":[65.22,794.63,314.58,807.97],"text":"– 16 –"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"","image":"2023-october-a-q52-choice-a.webp"},{"label":"b","text":"","image":"2023-october-a-q52-choice-b.webp"},{"label":"c","text":"","image":"2023-october-a-q52-choice-c.webp"},{"label":"d","text":"","image":"2023-october-a-q52-choice-d.webp"}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'The correct graph is the S-shaped curve whose cumulative bug count levels off as testing continues. A flattening reliability growth curve means few new bugs are being found per test item, so quality is stabilizing. A curve that keeps rising steeply or in a straight line means bugs are still being found at a high or constant rate, and a cumulative count can never decrease.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'quality-assurance'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2023A, FE, Subject-A, Q52)',
