  'For blank A, address 18 is 000 100 10 in binary, so tag = 000, line = 100 and byte offset = 10; the word starting at address 16 (0A 00 1B 00) is loaded into line 100 with tag 000. For blank B, line 101 with tag 001 gives the address 001 101 00 = 52, so the word holds addresses 52 to 55 (34 to 37 in hex). For blank C, address 0 hits (line 000, tag 000). Address 32 (tag 001, line 000) misses and replaces that line, so address 2 (tag 000) then misses, and address 34 (tag 001) misses again: the remaining sequence is miss → miss. This back-and-forth replacement on the same line is the weakness of a direct-mapped cache. In Table 1, the address range is 0 to 2^(T+L+B) − 1 (blank D, since the full address has T+L+B bits), and the tag storage is 2^L lines × T bits = 2^L × T bits (blank E).',
