  'For blank A, each registration belongs to exactly one student, and a student has zero registrations (not yet registered) or one (one topic), so the line is "only one" on the Student side and "zero or one" on the Registration side. For blank B, each registration has exactly one instructor, while an instructor may supervise none or several students (up to 5), so the line is "only one" to "zero or many". For SQL1, blanks C and D are LEFT OUTER JOIN and r.ID IS NULL: the left join keeps every student, and students with no matching registration get NULL registration columns, which picks out S2104 Selena. An inner join would drop exactly the unregistered students you are looking for. For blank E, SQL3 joins each student to their faculty''s maximum grade, which is 90 for FIT and 85 for FEE, so three students win: S2102 and S2103 (tied at 90) and S2104.',
