  'For blank A, the ARP request is a broadcast and L2SW#1''s MAC table is still empty, so the frame arriving on port 1 is flooded to every other port: 2, 3, 4 and 5. Port 5 is the link to the router. Support2 is on a different subnet, so for blanks B, C and D Sales1 sends an ARP request to find the MAC address of the default gateway (192.168.10.1). For blank E, the frame Sales1 sends to Support1 carries the router G0 MAC address 00:00:5E:00:53:AA as its destination MAC, but the destination IP stays Support1''s 192.168.20.2. For blank F, when R1 forwards the packet it rewrites the frame header, so Support1 sees source MAC 00:00:5E:00:53:BB (the G1 interface), while the source IP is still Sales1''s 192.168.10.2. The key point is that MAC addresses change at each hop and IP addresses stay end-to-end.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'networking'),
