  'For blank A, in test case 1 a = 9 makes (a < 10) true, so (b < 20) is skipped by the short-cut "or"; c = 10 makes (c > 10) false, so (d > 10) is skipped by the short-cut "and". The answer is that b < 20 and d > 10 are not evaluated. For blank B, in test case 2 (a < 10) is false, so b must be checked, and (c > 10) is true, so d must be checked: all single conditions are evaluated. In the control flow graph, node 4 (b < 20) branches to node 5 or to node 6 (func3), so blank C is node 6, and node 7 (c > 10) leads to node 8 (d > 10), so blank D is node 8. For blank E, branch coverage needs 3 test cases: one with x ≤ 10, plus two with x > 10 that together make each inner IF both true and false. For blank F, the graph has 12 nodes (S, 1 to 10, E) and 16 edges, so P = 16 − 12 + 2 = 6.',
