(
  '2024-april',
  'A',
  8,
  'Q8. There are two microprocessors: a 32-bit CPU capable of executing each of 32-bit, 16-bit,
 and 8-bit long instructions in 1 cycle, and a 16-bit CPU capable of executing each of 16-bit
 and 8-bit long instructions in 1 cycle. Additionally, the 16-bit CPU can execute a 32-bit
 long instruction in 2 cycles. When the appearance ratio of 32-bit long instructions is 40%, of
 16-bit long instructions is 40%, and of 8-bit long instructions is 20% respectively, what is
 the percentage improvement in total execution time with the 32-bit CPU compared to the
 16-bit CPU? Here, both the 32-bit and 16-bit CPU operate at the same clock speed and have
 the same performance except for the executable instruction length in 1 cycle.',
  '[{"type":"text","page":6,"bbox":[65.22,183.6,522.37,197.05],"text":"Q8. There are two microprocessors: a 32-bit CPU capable of executing each of 32-bit, 16-bit,"},{"type":"text","page":6,"bbox":[65.22,201.77,532.99,341.05],"text":"and 8-bit long instructions in 1 cycle, and a 16-bit CPU capable of executing each of 16-bit\nand 8-bit long instructions in 1 cycle. Additionally, the 16-bit CPU can execute a 32-bit\nlong instruction in 2 cycles. When the appearance ratio of 32-bit long instructions is 40%, of\n16-bit long instructions is 40%, and of 8-bit long instructions is 20% respectively, what is\nthe percentage improvement in total execution time with the 32-bit CPU compared to the\n16-bit CPU? Here, both the 32-bit and 16-bit CPU operate at the same clock speed and have\nthe same performance except for the executable instruction length in 1 cycle."},{"type":"text","page":6,"bbox":[84.12,345.77,485.1,359.05],"text":"a) 28.57\nb) 40\nc) 50\nd) 71.43"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"28.57"},{"label":"b","text":"40"},{"label":"c","text":"50"},{"label":"d","text":"71.43"}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'The improvement is 28.57%. The 32-bit CPU needs 1 cycle for every instruction, so its average is 1 cycle; the 16-bit CPU needs 0.4×2 + 0.4×1 + 0.2×1 = 1.4 cycles on average. The execution time is reduced by (1.4 − 1)/1.4 ≈ 0.2857, i.e. 28.57% of the 16-bit CPU''s time; 40% would be the figure measured relative to the 32-bit CPU instead.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'computer-architecture'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2024S, FE, Subject-A, Q8)',
  ARRAY[6]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q8. There are two microprocessors: a 32-bit CPU capable of executing each of 32-bit, 16-bit,
 and 8-bit long instructions in 1 cycle, and a 16-bit CPU capable of executing each of 16-bit
