(
  '2024-april',
  'B',
  19,
  'Q19. From the answer group below, select the most appropriate combination of answers to be
 inserted into ***A*** and ***B*** in the following description.

Company Y is an online retailer providing e-commerce web applications for customers to
 make purchases online and also allows customers to post product reviews. The web
 application was developed and maintained in-house. The properties of the e-commerce web
 application are shown in Figure 1.

• The web application resides on the web server in the demilitarized zone (DMZ), and
 the database used by the web application resides on the database server in the internal
 network.
 • The firewall is properly configured, the customers can only access the web server in
 the DMZ, and the database can only be accessed from the web server.
 • The web server allows access only through the HTTPS service, and the certificate is
 properly configured.
 • The customers can login using their registered email address and password.
 • The password is hashed and stored on the database.
 • The customer is locked out and notified by email with a recovery link if there are 5
 consecutive incorrect login attempts.
 • Third-party payment gateways are used to perform financial transactions. The credit
 card information of customers is not stored in the database.
 • There is a password recovery system which allows the customer to reset the password
 through a password recovery link sent to the registered email address.

Figure 1 Properties of the e-commerce web application

Due to recent incidents with multiple customers reporting that their accounts were
 compromised, the management tasked the IT team to investigate and address the issues. The
 IT team found that many customer accounts logins were unnoticed by the actual account
 owners, and some of the accounts were used to post fake reviews. The IT team also
 discovered unauthorized access to the database with illegal queries executed with no trace
 of administration login.

The IT team concluded that the incidents are most likely caused by using the same password
 on other leaked sites and ***A***. They proposed to implement 2-factor authentication
 and ***B*** to mitigate respective issues in the future. The management then agreed to
 the plan, and the solutions was implemented accordingly.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q19. From the answer group below, select the most appropriate combination of answers to be\n inserted into ***A*** and ***B*** in the following description."},{"type":"context","text":"Company Y is an online retailer providing e-commerce web applications for customers to\n make purchases online and also allows customers to post product reviews. The web\n application was developed and maintained in-house. The properties of the e-commerce web\n application are shown in Figure 1.\n\n• The web application resides on the web server in the demilitarized zone (DMZ), and\n the database used by the web application resides on the database server in the internal\n network.\n • The firewall is properly configured, the customers can only access the web server in\n the DMZ, and the database can only be accessed from the web server.\n • The web server allows access only through the HTTPS service, and the certificate is\n properly configured.\n • The customers can login using their registered email address and password.\n • The password is hashed and stored on the database.\n • The customer is locked out and notified by email with a recovery link if there are 5\n consecutive incorrect login attempts.\n • Third-party payment gateways are used to perform financial transactions. The credit\n card information of customers is not stored in the database.\n • There is a password recovery system which allows the customer to reset the password\n through a password recovery link sent to the registered email address.\n\nFigure 1 Properties of the e-commerce web application\n\nDue to recent incidents with multiple customers reporting that their accounts were\n compromised, the management tasked the IT team to investigate and address the issues. The\n IT team found that many customer accounts logins were unnoticed by the actual account\n owners, and some of the accounts were used to post fake reviews. The IT team also\n discovered unauthorized access to the database with illegal queries executed with no trace\n of administration login.\n\nThe IT team concluded that the incidents are most likely caused by using the same password\n on other leaked sites and ***A***. They proposed to implement 2-factor authentication\n and ***B*** to mitigate respective issues in the future. The management then agreed to\n the plan, and the solutions was implemented accordingly."},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"connection tapping","B":"database encryption"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"connection tapping","B":"web application firewall"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"online brute force attacks","B":"CAPTCHA"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"online brute force attacks","B":"more complex password policy"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"SQL injection","B":"database encryption"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"SQL injection","B":"web application firewall"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"connection tapping database encryption","values":{"A":"connection tapping","B":"database encryption"}},{"label":"b","text":"connection tapping web application firewall","values":{"A":"connection tapping","B":"web application firewall"}},{"label":"c","text":"online brute force attacks CAPTCHA","values":{"A":"online brute force attacks","B":"CAPTCHA"}},{"label":"d","text":"online brute force attacks more complex password policy","values":{"A":"online brute force attacks","B":"more complex password policy"}},{"label":"e","text":"SQL injection database encryption","values":{"A":"SQL injection","B":"database encryption"}},{"label":"f","text":"SQL injection web application firewall","values":{"A":"SQL injection","B":"web application firewall"}}]'::jsonb,
  'f',
  '{"value":"f"}'::jsonb,
  'The answer is A = SQL injection and B = web application firewall. The database can only be reached from the web server, yet illegal queries ran with no administrator login, which points to malicious input sent through the web application (SQL injection); account takeovers from reused passwords are handled separately by 2-factor authentication. A WAF inspects HTTP requests and blocks injection payloads, whereas database encryption would not help because the application itself decrypts the data when the injected query runs.',
