(
  '2024-april',
  'B',
  20,
  'Q20. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** through ***C*** in the description.

A zone is a group of interfaces that have similar functions or features. Zones establish the
 security borders of a network. A zone defines a boundary where traffic is subjected to policy
 restrictions when crossing into another region of a network. An inspection policy is applied
 to traffic moving between zones. Inter-zone policies offer considerable flexibility. Hence,
 different inspection policies can be applied to multiple host groups connected to the same
 router interface.

Company Z, intends to apply a zone-based policy firewall in their datacenter. Thus, they
 develop the network topology shown in Figure 1.

PUBLIC zone
 PRIVATE zone
 The Internet
 PC-1

・・・ DMZ zone

PC-n Server-1

・・・

Server-n

Figure 1 Network topology

These zones have the following characteristics:
 • The DMZ zone represents a zone where servers accessed from the Internet are
 located. Even if a server in the DMZ zone is breached, direct access to the internal
 PCs of the datacenter is prevented.

• The PUBLIC zone represents the entire network outside the datacenter.

• The PRIVATE zone represents the internal network. All datacenter PCs are located
 in this zone.

The security policy for the datacenter should be:
 • Hosts in the ***A*** zone cannot connect to hosts in the ***B*** zone.

• Hosts in the ***A*** zone can only access the DNS service on all hosts in the
 ***C*** zone and HTTP/HTTPS service on limited hosts in the ***C*** zone
 to retrieve software updates.

• Hosts in the ***B*** zone can connect to hosts in the ***A*** zone on all
 TCP, UDP and ICMP services.

• Hosts in the ***B*** zone can reach the ***C*** zone but not vice versa.

• Hosts in the ***C*** zone can reach HTTPS service on hosts in the ***A***
 zone. This policy will restrict access to other services available on each server.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q20. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** through ***C*** in the description."},{"type":"context","text":"A zone is a group of interfaces that have similar functions or features. Zones establish the\n security borders of a network. A zone defines a boundary where traffic is subjected to policy\n restrictions when crossing into another region of a network. An inspection policy is applied\n to traffic moving between zones. Inter-zone policies offer considerable flexibility. Hence,\n different inspection policies can be applied to multiple host groups connected to the same\n router interface.\n\nCompany Z, intends to apply a zone-based policy firewall in their datacenter. Thus, they\n develop the network topology shown in Figure 1.\n\nPUBLIC zone\n PRIVATE zone\n The Internet\n PC-1\n\n・・・ DMZ zone\n\nPC-n Server-1\n\n・・・\n\nServer-n\n\nFigure 1 Network topology\n\nThese zones have the following characteristics:\n • The DMZ zone represents a zone where servers accessed from the Internet are\n located. Even if a server in the DMZ zone is breached, direct access to the internal\n PCs of the datacenter is prevented.\n\n• The PUBLIC zone represents the entire network outside the datacenter.\n\n• The PRIVATE zone represents the internal network. All datacenter PCs are located\n in this zone.\n\nThe security policy for the datacenter should be:\n • Hosts in the ***A*** zone cannot connect to hosts in the ***B*** zone.\n\n• Hosts in the ***A*** zone can only access the DNS service on all hosts in the\n ***C*** zone and HTTP/HTTPS service on limited hosts in the ***C*** zone\n to retrieve software updates.\n\n• Hosts in the ***B*** zone can connect to hosts in the ***A*** zone on all\n TCP, UDP and ICMP services.\n\n• Hosts in the ***B*** zone can reach the ***C*** zone but not vice versa.\n\n• Hosts in the ***C*** zone can reach HTTPS service on hosts in the ***A***\n zone. This policy will restrict access to other services available on each server."},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B","C"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"DMZ","B":"PUBLIC","C":"PRIVATE"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"DMZ","B":"PRIVATE","C":"PUBLIC"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"PRIVATE","B":"DMZ","C":"PUBLIC"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"PRIVATE","B":"PUBLIC","C":"DMZ"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"PUBLIC","B":"DMZ","C":"PRIVATE"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"PUBLIC","B":"PRIVATE","C":"DMZ"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"DMZ PUBLIC PRIVATE","values":{"A":"DMZ","B":"PUBLIC","C":"PRIVATE"}},{"label":"b","text":"DMZ PRIVATE PUBLIC","values":{"A":"DMZ","B":"PRIVATE","C":"PUBLIC"}},{"label":"c","text":"PRIVATE DMZ PUBLIC","values":{"A":"PRIVATE","B":"DMZ","C":"PUBLIC"}},{"label":"d","text":"PRIVATE PUBLIC DMZ","values":{"A":"PRIVATE","B":"PUBLIC","C":"DMZ"}},{"label":"e","text":"PUBLIC DMZ PRIVATE","values":{"A":"PUBLIC","B":"DMZ","C":"PRIVATE"}},{"label":"f","text":"PUBLIC PRIVATE DMZ\n\n_ _\nCompany names and product names appearing in the test questions are trademarks or registered\ntrademarks of their respective companies. Note that the ® and ™ symbols are not used within the text.","values":{"A":"PUBLIC","B":"PRIVATE","C":"DMZ"}}]'::jsonb,
  'b',
  '{"value":"b"}'::jsonb,
  'The answer is A = PUBLIC, B = PRIVATE, C = DMZ. Internet hosts (PUBLIC) must never connect directly to internal PCs (PRIVATE) and may only reach limited services in the DMZ, while internal PCs may connect out to the Internet freely. The PRIVATE zone may reach the DMZ but not the other way round, which matches the stated purpose that a breached DMZ server cannot directly access internal PCs, and DMZ servers may reach HTTPS on the Internet, for example to fetch updates.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'network-security'),
