  'Q3. Which of the following is the computational complexity of the Heapsort algorithm? Here,
 n is the number of elements to be sorted, and all comparisons, swaps, and other needed
 operations can proceed in constant time.',
  '[{"type":"text","page":4,"bbox":[65.22,431.93,530.55,463.21],"text":"Q3.  Which of the following is the computational complexity of the Heapsort algorithm? Here,"},{"type":"text","page":4,"bbox":[85.74,467.93,506.0,499.21],"text":"n is the number of elements to be sorted, and all comparisons, swaps, and other needed\noperations can proceed in constant time."},{"type":"text","page":4,"bbox":[65.22,521.49,511.08,570.13],"text":"a) O (log n)\nb) O (n)\nc) O (n2)\nd) O (n log n)"},{"type":"code","page":4,"bbox":[65.22,794.63,311.58,807.97],"text":"– 4 –"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"O (log n)"},{"label":"b","text":"O (n)"},{"label":"c","text":"O (n²)"},{"label":"d","text":"O (n log n)"}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'Heapsort runs in O(n log n). Building the heap takes O(n), and then each of the n removals of the maximum requires a sift-down along the heap height, which is O(log n). Unlike quicksort, heapsort keeps O(n log n) even in the worst case, so O(n²) is wrong.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
