  4,
  'Q4. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program.

The procedure primeFactors outputs the prime factors for the input values as its argument.
 The first few prime numbers are 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, and 13. For instance, if the input integer is 12,
 the output is “2×2×3”. If the input integer is 78, the output is “2×3×13”. The input integer
 must be greater than 1.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q4. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program."},{"type":"context","text":"The procedure primeFactors outputs the prime factors for the input values as its argument.\n The first few prime numbers are 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, and 13. For instance, if the input integer is 12,\n the output is “2×2×3”. If the input integer is 78, the output is “2×3×13”. The input integer\n must be greater than 1."},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ primeFactors(integer: num)\n  integer: i\n\n  i ← 2\n  do\n    if (***A***)\n      num ← integer part of (num ÷ i)\n      output i\n      if (***B***)\n        output \"×\"\n      endif\n    else\n      i ← i + 1\n    endif\n  while (***B***)"},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"num < 1","B":"num mod i ≠ 0"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"num > 1","B":"num mod i = 0"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"num mod i = 0","B":"num > 1"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"num mod i ≠ 0","B":"num < 1"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"num < 1 num mod i ≠ 0","values":{"A":"num < 1","B":"num mod i ≠ 0"}},{"label":"b","text":"num > 1 num mod i = 0","values":{"A":"num > 1","B":"num mod i = 0"}},{"label":"c","text":"num mod i = 0 num > 1","values":{"A":"num mod i = 0","B":"num > 1"}},{"label":"d","text":"num mod i ≠ 0 num < 1","values":{"A":"num mod i ≠ 0","B":"num < 1"}}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'This is trial division: while i divides num (A: num mod i = 0), divide num by i and output i; otherwise try the next i. The loop must continue while factors remain (B: num > 1), and the same condition decides whether to print "×" after a factor, because a separator is needed only if more factors follow. Tracing 12: output 2 (num 6), ×, 2 (num 3), ×, then i becomes 3 and 3 is output with num = 1, giving 2×2×3.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2024A, FE, Subject-B, Q4)',
  ARRAY[7]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q4. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ___A___ and ___B___ in the program.

 The procedure primeFactors outputs the prime factors for the input values as its argument.
 The first few prime numbers are 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, and 13. For instance, if the input integer is 12,
 the output is “2×2×3”. If the input integer is 78, the output is “2×3×13”. The input integer
 must be greater than 1.

 [Program]
 ○ primeFactors(integer: num)
 integer: i

 i ← 2
 do
 if (___A___)
 num ← integer part of (num ÷ i)
 output i
 if (___B___)
 output "×"
 endif
 else
 i ← i + 1
 endif
 while (___B___)

 Answer group
 A B
 a) num < 1 num mod i ≠ 0
 b) num > 1 num mod i = 0
 c) num mod i = 0 num > 1
 d) num mod i ≠ 0 num < 1',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2024-october/b/questions.pdf","pages":[7]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2024-october/b/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
