  15,
  'Q15. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program. Here, the array indexes start at 1.

Suppose the weather in a city, from day to day, follows a Markov process. This implies the
 next day’s weather depends only on today’s weather and not on those of the previous days.
 If it rains today in the city, a probability 0.3 exists that it will rain again tomorrow. Similarly,
 the probability that tomorrow’s weather will be sunny is 0.4 and that it will be cloudy is 0.3.
 If today’s weather is sunny, the next day will be rainy, sunny, or cloudy with probabilities
 0.2, 0.7, and 0.1, respectively. Additionally, if today’s weather is cloudy, the next day will
 be rainy, sunny, or cloudy with probabilities 0.25, 0.5, and 0.25, respectively.
 Let us say that the weather is in state 1 if it is rainy, in state 2 if it is sunny, and in state 3 if
 it is cloudy. Then, the above is a three-state Markov chain whose transition probabilities are
 given by the following:

to
 1 2 3

1 0.3 0.4 0.3
 from 2 0.2 0.7 0.1
 3 0.25 0.5 0.25

Using this transition matrix, the probability distribution of the weather after several days can
 be estimated.
 The program calculates the probability distribution of the weather of the city after three days
 from the present day and outputs a 3 × 3 square matrix that shows this probability distribution
 of the weather. For instance, if the weather on the present day is sunny (state 2), the
 probability that the weather three days after will be rainy (state 1) is the value in the second
 row and the first column of the outputted matrix.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q15. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program. Here, the array indexes start at 1."},{"type":"context","text":"Suppose the weather in a city, from day to day, follows a Markov process. This implies the\n next day’s weather depends only on today’s weather and not on those of the previous days.\n If it rains today in the city, a probability 0.3 exists that it will rain again tomorrow. Similarly,\n the probability that tomorrow’s weather will be sunny is 0.4 and that it will be cloudy is 0.3.\n If today’s weather is sunny, the next day will be rainy, sunny, or cloudy with probabilities\n 0.2, 0.7, and 0.1, respectively. Additionally, if today’s weather is cloudy, the next day will\n be rainy, sunny, or cloudy with probabilities 0.25, 0.5, and 0.25, respectively.\n Let us say that the weather is in state 1 if it is rainy, in state 2 if it is sunny, and in state 3 if\n it is cloudy. Then, the above is a three-state Markov chain whose transition probabilities are\n given by the following:\n\nto\n 1 2 3\n\n1 0.3 0.4 0.3\n from 2 0.2 0.7 0.1\n 3 0.25 0.5 0.25\n\nUsing this transition matrix, the probability distribution of the weather after several days can\n be estimated.\n The program calculates the probability distribution of the weather of the city after three days\n from the present day and outputs a 3 × 3 square matrix that shows this probability distribution\n of the weather. For instance, if the weather on the present day is sunny (state 2), the\n probability that the weather three days after will be rainy (state 1) is the value in the second\n row and the first column of the outputted matrix."},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"2","B":"dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"2","B":"dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"3","B":"dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"3","B":"dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"4","B":"dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"4","B":"dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '["2024-october-b-q15-figure1.webp","2024-october-b-q15-figure2.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"2 dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]","values":{"A":"2","B":"dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]"}},{"label":"b","text":"2 dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]","values":{"A":"2","B":"dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]"}},{"label":"c","text":"3 dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]","values":{"A":"3","B":"dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]"}},{"label":"d","text":"3 dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]","values":{"A":"3","B":"dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]"}},{"label":"e","text":"4 dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]","values":{"A":"4","B":"dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]"}},{"label":"f","text":"4 dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]","values":{"A":"4","B":"dmat[k, i] × pmat[j, k]"}}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'The three-day transition matrix is P³, and dmat already starts as P¹, so it must be multiplied by pmat twice: A is 2. Each pass is ordinary matrix multiplication, where element [i, j] of the product is the sum over k of dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j] (row i of the left matrix times column j of the right), so B is dmat[i, k] × pmat[k, j]. Choosing 3 would compute P⁴, the distribution after four days.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
