  'Q18. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into ______ in
 the description.

Company B runs several web services. Mr. Y is a software engineer in the company. He was
 directed to develop a feature that prevents web service users from setting weak passwords.
 Mr. Y investigated and found that “Password Entropy” is the important factor for the
 password strength.

Password entropy measures the strength of a password based on the difficulty of cracking
 the password through guessing or a brute-force attack. The entropy of a password is typically
 based on the type of characters used—lowercase letters, uppercase letters, numerical digits,
 or special characters—and the length of the password or total number of characters.

Password entropy formula:
 You can measure the entropy of a password in bits using the following formula:

E = ⌊L ×Log2 (R)⌋

In this formula:
 - E denotes the password entropy
 - R denotes the possible range of character types in the password
 - L denotes the number of characters in the password (its length)
 - Log2 answers the question, "to what power 2 must be raised to equal this number"
 - ⌊X⌋ denotes the greatest integer N such that N ≤ X

The following totals show the breakdown for each character set as they appear on a typical
 American QWERTY keyboard:
 Lowercase letters (a–z) = 26.
 Uppercase letters (A–Z) = 26.
 Numerical digits (0–9) = 10.
 Special characters (!, @, #, $, %, ^, etc.) = 32.

The password vfssxfrb has a pool of 26 characters (lowercase letters);
 Changing the password to JAAdMAdE would increase the pool to 52 characters (lowercase
 letters and uppercase letters);
 Changing it further to u7aHqsbt would increase the pool to 62 characters (lowercase letters,
 uppercase letters along with numbers) and
 Finally, Re*ct7$% has a pool of 26 + 26 + 10 + 32 = 94 characters (lowercase letters,

uppercase letters, numbers, and special characters).

In our example:

• For vfssxfrb, we have R = 26 and L = 8; thus,
 E = ⌊8 × log2(26) ⌋ ≈ ⌊8 × 4.700⌋ ≈ ⌊37.60⌋ = 37 bits

• For JAAdMAdE, we have R = 52 and L = 8; thus,
 E = ⌊8 × log2(52) ⌋ ≈ ⌊8 × 5.700⌋ ≈ ⌊45.60⌋ = 45 bits

• For u7aHqsbt, we have R = 62 and L = 8; thus,
 E = ⌊8 × log2(62) ⌋ ≈ ⌊8 × 5.954⌋ ≈ ⌊47.63⌋ = 47 bits

• For Re*ct7$%, we have R = 94 and L = 8; thus,
 E = ⌊8 × log2(94) ⌋ ≈ ⌊8 × 6.555⌋ ≈ ⌊52.44⌋ = 52 bits

Here, when the password is A6GmyVyOC (Using numerical digits, lowercase letters, and
uppercase letters. L = 9), the password entropy is ____ ___ bits.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q18. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into ______ in\n the description."},{"type":"context","text":"Company B runs several web services. Mr. Y is a software engineer in the company. He was\n directed to develop a feature that prevents web service users from setting weak passwords.\n Mr. Y investigated and found that “Password Entropy” is the important factor for the\n password strength.\n\nPassword entropy measures the strength of a password based on the difficulty of cracking\n the password through guessing or a brute-force attack. The entropy of a password is typically\n based on the type of characters used—lowercase letters, uppercase letters, numerical digits,\n or special characters—and the length of the password or total number of characters.\n\nPassword entropy formula:\n You can measure the entropy of a password in bits using the following formula:\n\nE = ⌊L ×Log2 (R)⌋\n\nIn this formula:\n - E denotes the password entropy\n - R denotes the possible range of character types in the password\n - L denotes the number of characters in the password (its length)\n - Log2 answers the question, \"to what power 2 must be raised to equal this number\"\n - ⌊X⌋ denotes the greatest integer N such that N ≤ X\n\nThe following totals show the breakdown for each character set as they appear on a typical\n American QWERTY keyboard:\n Lowercase letters (a–z) = 26.\n Uppercase letters (A–Z) = 26.\n Numerical digits (0–9) = 10.\n Special characters (!, @, #, $, %, ^, etc.) = 32.\n\nThe password vfssxfrb has a pool of 26 characters (lowercase letters);\n Changing the password to JAAdMAdE would increase the pool to 52 characters (lowercase\n letters and uppercase letters);\n Changing it further to u7aHqsbt would increase the pool to 62 characters (lowercase letters,\n uppercase letters along with numbers) and\n Finally, Re*ct7$% has a pool of 26 + 26 + 10 + 32 = 94 characters (lowercase letters,\n\nuppercase letters, numbers, and special characters).\n\nIn our example:\n\n• For vfssxfrb, we have R = 26 and L = 8; thus,\n E = ⌊8 × log2(26) ⌋ ≈ ⌊8 × 4.700⌋ ≈ ⌊37.60⌋ = 37 bits\n\n• For JAAdMAdE, we have R = 52 and L = 8; thus,\n E = ⌊8 × log2(52) ⌋ ≈ ⌊8 × 5.700⌋ ≈ ⌊45.60⌋ = 45 bits\n\n• For u7aHqsbt, we have R = 62 and L = 8; thus,\n E = ⌊8 × log2(62) ⌋ ≈ ⌊8 × 5.954⌋ ≈ ⌊47.63⌋ = 47 bits\n\n• For Re*ct7$%, we have R = 94 and L = 8; thus,\n E = ⌊8 × log2(94) ⌋ ≈ ⌊8 × 6.555⌋ ≈ ⌊52.44⌋ = 52 bits\n\nHere, when the password is A6GmyVyOC (Using numerical digits, lowercase letters, and\nuppercase letters. L = 9), the password entropy is ____ ___ bits."}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"42"},{"label":"b","text":"51"},{"label":"c","text":"52"},{"label":"d","text":"53"},{"label":"e","text":"58"}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'A6GmyVyOC uses digits, lowercase and uppercase letters, so R = 10 + 26 + 26 = 62, and L = 9. Then E = ⌊9 × log2(62)⌋ ≈ ⌊9 × 5.954⌋ = ⌊53.59⌋ = 53 bits. Forgetting the digits (R = 52) would give ⌊9 × 5.700⌋ = 51, which is a tempting wrong choice.',
