  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[4]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-april',
  'A',
  4,
  'Q4. A vehicle travels along a circular path as shown in the figure below. Two sensors, L and
 R, whose revolution speeds are independently controlled, are attached to the left and right
 rear wheels. Sensors L and R are photo-reflective sensors, and their values change according
 to the ratio of black and white regions they pass over. If the ratio increases, the sensor value
 decreases, and if the ratio decreases, the sensor value increases. Which of the following is
 an appropriate control to ensure that the vehicle travels along the black outline of the circle?
 Here, if the values of the two sensors are equal, the motor revolution speeds return to the
 initial values.',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q4. A vehicle travels along a circular path as shown in the figure below. Two sensors, L and\n R, whose revolution speeds are independently controlled, are attached to the left and right\n rear wheels. Sensors L and R are photo-reflective sensors, and their values change according\n to the ratio of black and white regions they pass over. If the ratio increases, the sensor value\n decreases, and if the ratio decreases, the sensor value increases. Which of the following is\n an appropriate control to ensure that the vehicle travels along the black outline of the circle?\n Here, if the values of the two sensors are equal, the motor revolution speeds return to the\n initial values."}]'::jsonb,
  '["2025-april-a-q04-figure1.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"Sensor L value > Sensor R value: Motor L revolution speed Decrease, Motor R revolution speed Increase; Sensor L value < Sensor R value: Motor L revolution speed Decrease, Motor R revolution speed Increase"},{"label":"b","text":"Sensor L value > Sensor R value: Motor L revolution speed Decrease, Motor R revolution speed Increase; Sensor L value < Sensor R value: Motor L revolution speed Increase, Motor R revolution speed Decrease"},{"label":"c","text":"Sensor L value > Sensor R value: Motor L revolution speed Increase, Motor R revolution speed Decrease; Sensor L value < Sensor R value: Motor L revolution speed Decrease, Motor R revolution speed Increase"},{"label":"d","text":"Sensor L value > Sensor R value: Motor L revolution speed Increase, Motor R revolution speed Decrease; Sensor L value < Sensor R value: Motor L revolution speed Increase, Motor R revolution speed Decrease"}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'The correct control is: when sensor L > sensor R, increase motor L and decrease motor R; when sensor L < sensor R, decrease motor L and increase motor R. The sensors straddle the black line, and a higher value means less black under the sensor. If L reads higher, the vehicle has drifted left off the line, so the left wheel must speed up and the right wheel slow down to steer back to the right, and the opposite applies when R reads higher.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'computer-architecture'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-A, Q4)',
  ARRAY[5]::int[],
