(
  '2025-april',
  'A',
  6,
  'Q6. In the graph shown below, which of the following is the output of a depth-first traversal
 starting from vertex A, and visiting all vertices in ascending character order?',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q6. In the graph shown below, which of the following is the output of a depth-first traversal\n starting from vertex A, and visiting all vertices in ascending character order?"}]'::jsonb,
  '["2025-april-a-q06-figure1.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"A B C D E F"},{"label":"b","text":"A B C D F E"},{"label":"c","text":"A B D E F C"},{"label":"d","text":"A B D F E C"}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'The official answer A B D F E C comes from a stack-based depth-first search: when a vertex is visited, its unvisited neighbours are pushed in ascending order (and marked), so the last one pushed is popped next. From A push B; from B push C then D, so D is popped; from D push E then F, so F is popped; F''s neighbours are already marked, so E and then C are popped, giving A B D F E C. Note that a recursive DFS that always moves to the smallest unvisited neighbour would give A B C F D E, which is not among the choices, so the question expects the stack-based reading.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'data-structures'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-A, Q6)',
  ARRAY[6]::int[],
  'manual_review',
  '3.0',
  'Q6. In the graph shown below, which of the following is the output of a depth-first traversal
 starting from vertex A, and visiting all vertices in ascending character order?

 A D E

 B C F

 a) A B C D E F b) A B C D F E
 c) A B D E F C d) A B D F E C',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[6]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-april',
  'A',
  7,
  'Q7. A hash table of size 11 uses open addressing with hash function h(k)=k mod 11, and linear
 probing. The table shown below is created after inserting 4 values into an initially empty
 hash table. Which of the following is the sequence that represents a possible order in
 which the key values were inserted into the table?

 0
 1
 2 79
 3 14
 4 35
 5 92
 6
 7
 8
 9
 10',
  '[{"type":"text","page":7,"bbox":[65.22,67.74,530.34,81.19],"text":"Q7. A hash table of size 11 uses open addressing with hash function h(k)=k mod 11, and linear"},{"type":"text","page":7,"bbox":[85.62,85.85,518.07,135.13],"text":"probing. The table shown below is created after inserting 4 values into an initially empty\nhash table. Which of the following is the sequence that represents a possible order in\nwhich the key values were inserted into the table?"},{"type":"table","page":7,"bbox":[150.3,156.93,228.3,313.78],"rows":[["","0","","",null,null],["","1","","",null,null],["","2","","","79",""],["","3","","","14",""],["","4","","","35",""],["","5","","","92",""],["","6","","",null,null],["","7","","",null,null],["","8","","",null,null],["","9","","",null,null],["","10","","",null,null]]},{"type":"text","page":7,"bbox":[65.22,158.88,516.96,364.03],"text":"0\n\n1\n\n2\n79\n3\n14\n4\n35\n5\n92\n6\n\n7\n\n8\n\n9\n\n10\n\n\na) 14, 79, 35, 92\nb) 79, 14, 92, 35\nc) 79, 92, 35, 14\nd) 92, 79, 14, 35"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"14, 79, 35, 92"},{"label":"b","text":"79, 14, 92, 35"},{"label":"c","text":"79, 92, 35, 14"},{"label":"d","text":"92, 79, 14, 35"}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'The order 14, 79, 35, 92 is possible. The home slots are 79 mod 11 = 2, 14 mod 11 = 3, 35 mod 11 = 2 and 92 mod 11 = 4. For 35 to end in slot 4, slots 2 and 3 must already hold 79 and 14 while slot 4 is still empty, and 92 must come after 35 so it collides at 4 and moves to 5. Any order that inserts 92 before 35 would put 92 in slot 4 instead.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'data-structures'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
