 FROM OrderDetails

 ___A___',
  '[{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[65.22,67.26,516.45,80.71],"text":"Q21.\nWhich of the following is the clause that should be inserted into blank ___A___ of"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[85.62,85.85,517.47,117.13],"text":"the SQL statement below in order to display the total sales data for each product that sold\nfor at least $5,000 from the table OrderDetails?"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[65.22,133.85,358.71,147.13],"text":"OrderDetails (OrderID, ProductID, UnitPrice, Quantity)"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[65.22,163.85,168.86,177.13],"text":"[SQL statement]"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[137.22,183.25,402.5,193.27],"text":"SELECT ProductID, SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity)"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[137.22,199.68,251.86,211.68],"text":"FROM OrderDetails"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[138.72,217.43,193.92,230.71],"text":"___A___"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[84.12,254.93,466.56,322.21],"text":"a) GROUP BY ProductID HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;\nb) HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;\nc) HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000 GROUP BY ProductI;\nd) WHERE SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"GROUP BY ProductID HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;"},{"label":"b","text":"HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;"},{"label":"c","text":"HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000 GROUP BY ProductI;"},{"label":"d","text":"WHERE SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;"}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'The correct clause is GROUP BY ProductID HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000. GROUP BY creates one row per product so SUM gives each product''s total, and HAVING filters those groups by the aggregate. WHERE cannot contain an aggregate function, and HAVING must come after GROUP BY, not before it.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'databases'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-A, Q21)',
  ARRAY[12]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q21. Which of the following is the clause that should be inserted into blank ___A___ of
 the SQL statement below in order to display the total sales data for each product that sold
 for at least $5,000 from the table OrderDetails?

 OrderDetails (OrderID, ProductID, UnitPrice, Quantity)

 [SQL statement]

 SELECT ProductID, SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity)

 FROM OrderDetails

 ___A___

 a) GROUP BY ProductID HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;
 b) HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;
 c) HAVING SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000 GROUP BY ProductI;
 d) WHERE SUM(UnitPrice * Quantity) >= 5000;',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[12]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-april',
  'A',
  22,
  'Q22. Which of the following is an appropriate explanation of the granularity of locks?',
  '[{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[65.22,361.74,497.41,375.19],"text":"Q22.\nWhich of the following is an appropriate explanation of the granularity of locks?"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[84.12,400.91,527.57,414.19],"text":"a) When large granularity is set for a large number of transactions to update the same data,"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[84.12,418.91,531.93,450.19],"text":"the number of transactions that can be executed simultaneously increases.\nb) When large granularity is set for all data in a table to be referenced, data referenced from"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[84.12,454.91,484.72,486.19],"text":"other transactions can remain undisturbed.\nc) When large granularity is set for data to be updated, the waiting times for other"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[102.12,490.91,370.99,504.19],"text":"transactions increase, and the total throughput declines."},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[84.12,508.91,501.36,522.19],"text":"d) When large granularity is set, the number of data items included increases, and the"},{"type":"text","page":12,"bbox":[65.22,526.91,363.69,558.13],"text":"number of locks that one transaction makes increases."}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"When large granularity is set for a large number of transactions to update the same data,\n the number of transactions that can be executed simultaneously increases."},{"label":"b","text":"When large granularity is set for all data in a table to be referenced, data referenced from\n other transactions can remain undisturbed."},{"label":"c","text":"When large granularity is set for data to be updated, the waiting times for other\n transactions increase, and the total throughput declines."},{"label":"d","text":"When large granularity is set, the number of data items included increases, and the\n number of locks that one transaction makes increases."}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'With large lock granularity (for example locking a whole table), each lock covers much more data, so other transactions wait longer and overall throughput declines. Large granularity actually reduces concurrency and reduces the number of locks a transaction needs, which is why the other statements are wrong.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'databases'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-A, Q22)',
  ARRAY[12]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q22. Which of the following is an appropriate explanation of the granularity of locks?

 a) When large granularity is set for a large number of transactions to update the same data,
 the number of transactions that can be executed simultaneously increases.
 b) When large granularity is set for all data in a table to be referenced, data referenced from
 other transactions can remain undisturbed.
 c) When large granularity is set for data to be updated, the waiting times for other
 transactions increase, and the total throughput declines.
 d) When large granularity is set, the number of data items included increases, and the
 number of locks that one transaction makes increases.',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/a/questions.pdf","pages":[12]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
