(
  '2025-april',
  'B',
  1,
  'Q1. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** through ***C*** in the program.

Centurial years refer to the years that are divisible by 100. The centurial years are not leap
 years except for years that are exactly divisible by 400. The non-centurial years, that is, years
 that are not divisible by 100, refer to leap years that are divisible by 4. The function
 isLeapYear receives an integer number year and returns true if a given year is a leap year
 or false otherwise.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q1. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** through ***C*** in the program."},{"type":"context","text":"Centurial years refer to the years that are divisible by 100. The centurial years are not leap\n years except for years that are exactly divisible by 400. The non-centurial years, that is, years\n that are not divisible by 100, refer to leap years that are divisible by 4. The function\n isLeapYear receives an integer number year and returns true if a given year is a leap year\n or false otherwise."},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"elseif (***C***)\n    return false\n  elseif (year mod 4 = 0)\n    return true\n  else\n    return false\n  endif"},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B","C"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"year mod 100 = 0","B":"false","C":"year mod 400 = 0"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"year mod 100 = 0","B":"true","C":"year mod 400 = 0"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"year mod 100 ≠ 0","B":"false","C":"year mod 400 ≠ 0"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"year mod 100 ≠ 0","B":"true","C":"year mod 400 ≠ 0"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"year mod 400 = 0","B":"false","C":"year mod 100 = 0"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"year mod 400 = 0","B":"true","C":"year mod 100 = 0"}},{"label":"g","values":{"A":"year mod 400 ≠ 0","B":"false","C":"year mod 100 ≠ 0"}},{"label":"h","values":{"A":"year mod 400 ≠ 0","B":"true","C":"year mod 100 ≠ 0"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"year mod 100 = 0 false year mod 400 = 0","values":{"A":"year mod 100 = 0","B":"false","C":"year mod 400 = 0"}},{"label":"b","text":"year mod 100 = 0 true year mod 400 = 0","values":{"A":"year mod 100 = 0","B":"true","C":"year mod 400 = 0"}},{"label":"c","text":"year mod 100 ≠ 0 false year mod 400 ≠ 0","values":{"A":"year mod 100 ≠ 0","B":"false","C":"year mod 400 ≠ 0"}},{"label":"d","text":"year mod 100 ≠ 0 true year mod 400 ≠ 0","values":{"A":"year mod 100 ≠ 0","B":"true","C":"year mod 400 ≠ 0"}},{"label":"e","text":"year mod 400 = 0 false year mod 100 = 0","values":{"A":"year mod 400 = 0","B":"false","C":"year mod 100 = 0"}},{"label":"f","text":"year mod 400 = 0 true year mod 100 = 0","values":{"A":"year mod 400 = 0","B":"true","C":"year mod 100 = 0"}},{"label":"g","text":"year mod 400 ≠ 0 false year mod 100 ≠ 0","values":{"A":"year mod 400 ≠ 0","B":"false","C":"year mod 100 ≠ 0"}},{"label":"h","text":"year mod 400 ≠ 0 true year mod 100 ≠ 0","values":{"A":"year mod 400 ≠ 0","B":"true","C":"year mod 100 ≠ 0"}}]'::jsonb,
  'f',
  '{"value":"f"}'::jsonb,
  'The most specific rule must be tested first: A is year mod 400 = 0 and B is true, because any year divisible by 400 is a leap year. C is year mod 100 = 0 (return false), since the remaining centurial years are not leap years; only then does the year mod 4 = 0 test apply to ordinary years. If the mod 100 test came first, years like 2000 would wrongly return false.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-B, Q1)',
  ARRAY[4]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q1. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ____A____ through ____C____ in the program.

 Centurial years refer to the years that are divisible by 100. The centurial years are not leap
 years except for years that are exactly divisible by 400. The non-centurial years, that is, years
 that are not divisible by 100, refer to leap years that are divisible by 4. The function
 isLeapYear receives an integer number year and returns true if a given year is a leap year
 or false otherwise.

 [Program]
 ○ boolean: isLeapYear(integer: year) // returns true if the variable year
 // is a leap year; otherwise, returns false
 if (____A____)
 return ____B____
 elseif (____C____)
 return false
 elseif (year mod 4 = 0)
 return true
 else
 return false
 endif

 Answer group
 A B C
 a) year mod 100 = 0 false year mod 400 = 0
 b) year mod 100 = 0 true year mod 400 = 0
 c) year mod 100 ≠ 0 false year mod 400 ≠ 0
 d) year mod 100 ≠ 0 true year mod 400 ≠ 0
 e) year mod 400 = 0 false year mod 100 = 0
 f) year mod 400 = 0 true year mod 100 = 0
 g) year mod 400 ≠ 0 false year mod 100 ≠ 0
 h) year mod 400 ≠ 0 true year mod 100 ≠ 0',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/questions.pdf","pages":[4]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-april',
  'B',
  2,
  'Q2. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program.

The function isPerfect receives positive number n, and returns whether n is a perfect
 number. Here, a number is a “perfect number” if the sum of its positive divisors (excluding
 the number itself) is equal to the number itself. For instance, 28 is a perfect number because
 28 has divisors 1, 2, 4, 7, and 14, and 1 + 2 + 4 + 7 + 14 = 28.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q2. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program."},{"type":"context","text":"The function isPerfect receives positive number n, and returns whether n is a perfect\n number. Here, a number is a “perfect number” if the sum of its positive divisors (excluding\n the number itself) is equal to the number itself. For instance, 28 is a perfect number because\n 28 has divisors 1, 2, 4, 7, and 14, and 1 + 2 + 4 + 7 + 14 = 28."},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ boolean: isPerfect(integer: n)\n  integer: k\n  integer: sum ← 0\n  integer: half ← integer part of (n ÷ 2)\n  for (increase k from 1 to half by 1)\n    if (***A***)\n      ***B***\n    endif\n  endfor\n  if (sum = n)\n    return true\n  else\n    return false\n  endif\n\nAnswer group"},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"n mod k ≠ 0","B":"←\nsum sum + 1"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"n mod k ≠ 0","B":"←\nsum sum + k"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"n mod k = 0","B":"←\nsum sum + 1"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"n mod k = 0","B":"←\nsum sum + k"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"n mod k ≠ 0 sum ← sum + 1","values":{"A":"n mod k ≠ 0","B":"←\nsum sum + 1"}},{"label":"b","text":"n mod k ≠ 0 sum ← sum + k","values":{"A":"n mod k ≠ 0","B":"←\nsum sum + k"}},{"label":"c","text":"n mod k = 0 sum ← sum + 1","values":{"A":"n mod k = 0","B":"←\nsum sum + 1"}},{"label":"d","text":"n mod k = 0 sum ← sum + k","values":{"A":"n mod k = 0","B":"←\nsum sum + k"}}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'The loop tries every candidate k from 1 to n ÷ 2 (no proper divisor can exceed half of n), so A must test that k is a divisor, n mod k = 0. B must add the divisor itself, sum ← sum + k, so that sum ends as the sum of proper divisors and can be compared with n. Adding 1 instead would only count the divisors, which gives 5 for 28 rather than 28.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-B, Q2)',
  ARRAY[5]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q2. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ___A___ and ___B___ in the program.

 The function isPerfect receives positive number n, and returns whether n is a perfect
 number. Here, a number is a “perfect number” if the sum of its positive divisors (excluding
 the number itself) is equal to the number itself. For instance, 28 is a perfect number because
 28 has divisors 1, 2, 4, 7, and 14, and 1 + 2 + 4 + 7 + 14 = 28.

 [Program]
 ○ boolean: isPerfect(integer: n)
 integer: k
 integer: sum ← 0
 integer: half ← integer part of (n ÷ 2)
 for (increase k from 1 to half by 1)
 if (___A___)
 ___B___
 endif
 endfor
 if (sum = n)
 return true
 else
 return false
 endif

 Answer group
 A B
 a) n mod k ≠ 0 sum ← sum + 1
 b) n mod k ≠ 0 sum ← sum + k
 c) n mod k = 0 sum ← sum + 1
 d) n mod k = 0 sum ← sum + k',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/questions.pdf","pages":[5]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-april',
  'B',
  3,
  'Q3. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into _______ in
 the description.

The greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers is the largest number that divides both
 of them. The function GCD receives two positive integer numbers and returns their GCD.
 When the function GCD is called as GCD(98, 56), the output is _______ in (1)–(4) below.
 Here, the output statement “output m, n” outputs the values of variables m and n, and
 subsequently starts a new line.

(1) 98 56 (2) 42 56 (3) 42 56 (4) 56 42
 42 14 42 14 42 28 42 14
 28 14 28 14 14 28 14 28
 14 7 14 14 14 14 14 14',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q3. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into _______ in\n the description."},{"type":"context","text":"The greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers is the largest number that divides both\n of them. The function GCD receives two positive integer numbers and returns their GCD.\n When the function GCD is called as GCD(98, 56), the output is _______ in (1)–(4) below.\n Here, the output statement “output m, n” outputs the values of variables m and n, and\n subsequently starts a new line.\n\n(1) 98 56 (2) 42 56 (3) 42 56 (4) 56 42\n 42 14 42 14 42 28 42 14\n 28 14 28 14 14 28 14 28\n 14 7 14 14 14 14 14 14"},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ integer: GCD(integer: x, integer: y)\n  integer: m ← x\n  integer: n ← y\n  while (m ≠ n)\n    if (m > n)\n      m ← m - n\n    else\n      n ← n - m\n    endif\n    output m, n\n  endwhile\n  return m\n\nAnswer group"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"(1)"},{"label":"b","text":"(2)"},{"label":"c","text":"(3)"},{"label":"d","text":"(4)"}]'::jsonb,
  'b',
  '{"value":"b"}'::jsonb,
  'The program is the subtraction form of Euclid''s algorithm and prints m, n after each subtraction, so the original pair 98 56 is never printed. Trace: 98 > 56 gives m = 42 (42 56); 42 < 56 gives n = 14 (42 14); then m = 28 (28 14); then m = 14 (14 14), and the loop stops with GCD 14. This is output (2); output (1) wrongly prints the starting values and continues past equality.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-B, Q3)',
  ARRAY[6]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q3. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into _______ in
 the description.

 The greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers is the largest number that divides both
 of them. The function GCD receives two positive integer numbers and returns their GCD.
 When the function GCD is called as GCD(98, 56), the output is _______ in (1)–(4) below.
 Here, the output statement “output m, n” outputs the values of variables m and n, and
 subsequently starts a new line.

 (1) 98 56 (2) 42 56 (3) 42 56 (4) 56 42
 42 14 42 14 42 28 42 14
 28 14 28 14 14 28 14 28
 14 7 14 14 14 14 14 14

 [Program]
 ○ integer: GCD(integer: x, integer: y)
 integer: m ← x
 integer: n ← y
 while (m ≠ n)
 if (m > n)
 m ← m - n
 else
 n ← n - m
 endif
 output m, n
 endwhile
 return m

 Answer group
 a) (1) b) (2) c) (3) d) (4)',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/questions.pdf","pages":[6]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-april',
  'B',
  4,
  'Q4. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program.

The following function receives an integer number and returns the result of interpreting the
 decimal representation of the number as a binary number. Note that the number is non-negative, and its decimal representation comprises only the digits 0 and 1. For instance, if it
 receives 1100, it returns 12.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q4. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program."},{"type":"context","text":"The following function receives an integer number and returns the result of interpreting the\n decimal representation of the number as a binary number. Note that the number is non-negative, and its decimal representation comprises only the digits 0 and 1. For instance, if it\n receives 1100, it returns 12."},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ integer: convert(integer: number)\n  integer: place, n, remainder, decimal\n  decimal ← 0\n  place ← 1\n  n ← number\n  while (n > 0)\n    remainder ← n mod 10\n    n ← integer part of (n ÷ 10)\n    decimal ← decimal + ***A***\n    place ← ***B***\n  endwhile\n  return decimal\n\nAnswer group"},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"2 × place","B":"n × place"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"2 × place","B":"remainder × place"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"10 × place","B":"2 × place"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"10 × place","B":"remainder × place"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"n × place","B":"2 × place"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"n × place","B":"remainder × place"}},{"label":"g","values":{"A":"remainder × place","B":"2 × place"}},{"label":"h","values":{"A":"remainder × place","B":"10 × place"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"2 × place n × place","values":{"A":"2 × place","B":"n × place"}},{"label":"b","text":"2 × place remainder × place","values":{"A":"2 × place","B":"remainder × place"}},{"label":"c","text":"10 × place 2 × place","values":{"A":"10 × place","B":"2 × place"}},{"label":"d","text":"10 × place remainder × place","values":{"A":"10 × place","B":"remainder × place"}},{"label":"e","text":"n × place 2 × place","values":{"A":"n × place","B":"2 × place"}},{"label":"f","text":"n × place remainder × place","values":{"A":"n × place","B":"remainder × place"}},{"label":"g","text":"remainder × place 2 × place","values":{"A":"remainder × place","B":"2 × place"}},{"label":"h","text":"remainder × place 10 × place","values":{"A":"remainder × place","B":"10 × place"}}]'::jsonb,
  'g',
  '{"value":"g"}'::jsonb,
  'Each pass takes the last decimal digit (remainder, 0 or 1) and gives it its binary place value, so A is remainder × place. The place value then doubles for the next digit, so B is 2 × place (1, 2, 4, 8, ...). For 1100 the digits from the right are 0, 0, 1, 1, giving 0×1 + 0×2 + 1×4 + 1×8 = 12; using 10 × place would just rebuild the decimal number.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-B, Q4)',
  ARRAY[7]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q4. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ___A___ and ___B___ in the program.

 The following function receives an integer number and returns the result of interpreting the
 decimal representation of the number as a binary number. Note that the number is non-negative, and its decimal representation comprises only the digits 0 and 1. For instance, if it
 receives 1100, it returns 12.

 [Program]
 ○ integer: convert(integer: number)
 integer: place, n, remainder, decimal
 decimal ← 0
 place ← 1
 n ← number
 while (n > 0)
 remainder ← n mod 10
 n ← integer part of (n ÷ 10)
 decimal ← decimal + ___A___
 place ← ___B___
 endwhile
 return decimal

 Answer group
 A B

 a) 2 × place n × place

 b) 2 × place remainder × place

 c) 10 × place 2 × place

 d) 10 × place remainder × place

 e) n × place 2 × place

 f) n × place remainder × place

 g) remainder × place 2 × place

 h) remainder × place 10 × place',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/questions.pdf","pages":[7]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-april',
  'B',
  5,
  'Q5. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into _________ in
 the program.

The function calc receives the positive real numbers x and y, and returns the result of the
 calculation of (√x + √y)². For instance, when the function calc is called as calc(4, 9),
 the return value is 25. Here, the function pow(a, b) returns a raised to the power of b.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q5. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into _________ in\n the program."},{"type":"context","text":"The function calc receives the positive real numbers x and y, and returns the result of the\n calculation of (√x + √y)². For instance, when the function calc is called as calc(4, 9),\n the return value is 25. Here, the function pow(a, b) returns a raised to the power of b."}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"pow(pow(x, 0.5) + pow(y, 0.5), 2)"},{"label":"b","text":"pow(pow(x, 0.5), 2) + pow(pow(y, 0.5), 2)"},{"label":"c","text":"pow(pow(x, 2) + pow(y, 2), 0.5)"},{"label":"d","text":"pow(x, 0.5) + pow(y, 0.5)"},{"label":"e","text":"pow(x, 0.5) + pow(y, 0.5) ÷ pow(2, 0.5)"},{"label":"f","text":"pow(x, 2) + pow(y, 2) ÷ pow(2, 0.5)"}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'A square root is a power of 0.5, so √x + √y is pow(x, 0.5) + pow(y, 0.5), and squaring the whole sum gives pow(pow(x, 0.5) + pow(y, 0.5), 2). Check: calc(4, 9) = (2 + 3)² = 25. Choice b squares each root separately and returns only x + y = 13, missing the cross term 2√x√y.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-B, Q5)',
  ARRAY[8]::int[],
  'manual_review',
  '3.0',
  'Q5. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into _________ in
 the program.

 The function calc receives the positive real numbers x and y, and returns the result of the
 calculation of (√x + ඥy )2. For instance, when the function calc is called as calc(4, 9),
 the return value is 25. Here, the function pow(a, b) returns a raised to the power of b.

 [Program]
 ○ real: calc(real: x, real: y)
 return _______

 Answer group
 a) pow(pow(x, 0.5) + pow(y, 0.5), 2)
 b) pow(pow(x, 0.5), 2) + pow(pow(y, 0.5), 2)
 c) pow(pow(x, 2) + pow(y, 2), 0.5)
 d) pow(x, 0.5) + pow(y, 0.5)
