  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q12. From the answer group below, select the correct answer to be inserted into _______ in\n the program. Here, the array index starts at 1."},{"type":"context","text":"The function hammingDistance compares the two-character arrays s1 and s2 that are given\n as arguments. s1 and s2 have one or more elements. If s1 and s2 do not have the same\n number of elements, the function returns -1. Otherwise, it returns the number of indices\n where two arrays have different element values at the same index. The figure shows an\n example of two character arrays. APPLE and APRIE have different element values at two\n indices.\n\nA P P L E\n A P R I E\n\nFigure Example of two-character arrays\n\nThe table lists examples of s1 and s2 given to the function hammingDistance and the return\n values. In the program, areas outside of the arrays must not be referenced.\n\nTable Examples of s1 and s2 given to the function hammingDistance\n\nand the return values\n\ns1 s2 Return value\n\n{\"a\", \"p\", \"p\", \"l\", \"e\"} {\"a\", \"p\", \"p\", \"l\", \"e\"} 0\n\n{\"a\", \"p\", \"p\", \"l\", \"e\"} {\"a\", \"p\", \"r\", \"i\", \"l\"} 3\n\n{\"a\", \"p\", \"p\", \"l\", \"e\"} {\"m\", \"e\", \"l\", \"o\", \"n\"} 5\n\n{\"a\", \"p\", \"p\", \"l\", \"e\"} {\"p\", \"i\", \"e\"} -1"},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ integer: hammingDistance(character []: s1, character []: s2)\n  integer: i, cnt ← 0\n  if (the number of elements in s1 ≠ the number of elements in s2)\n    return −1\n  endif\n  for (increase i from 1 to the number of elements in s1 by 1)\n    if (_______)\n      cnt ← cnt + 1\n    endif\n  endfor\n  return cnt"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"s1[cnt] = s2[cnt]"},{"label":"b","text":"s1[cnt] ≠ s2[cnt]"},{"label":"c","text":"s1[cnt] = s2[i]"},{"label":"d","text":"s1[cnt] ≠ s2[i]"},{"label":"e","text":"s1[i] = s2[cnt]"},{"label":"f","text":"s1[i] ≠ s2[cnt]"},{"label":"g","text":"s1[i] = s2[i]"},{"label":"h","text":"s1[i] ≠ s2[i]"}]'::jsonb,
  'h',
  '{"value":"h"}'::jsonb,
  'The Hamming distance counts positions where the two arrays differ, so for each index i the condition is s1[i] ≠ s2[i] and cnt is incremented. cnt is the running count, not a position, so expressions indexed by cnt would compare the wrong elements and could even read index 0. For apple and april the positions 3, 4 and 5 differ, giving 3 as in the table.',
