  'B',
  13,
  'Q13. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program. Here, the array index starts at 1.

The procedure maximumSubarray calculates the maximum sum of the subarray of the array
 T (the number of elements ≥ 1). A subarray is a contiguous portion of the array, for instance,
 from T[1] to T[3]. It can be as short as one element or as long as the entire array. The
 procedure finds one of the subarrays with the largest sum of values, and outputs the sum, the
 first, and the last indices of the subarray. For instance, if the content of the array is {-2, 1,
 -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4}, the output will be 6, 4, and 7 representing the sum, first index,
 and last index, respectively. Here, the subarray from T[4] to T[7] is {4, -1, 2, 1}.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q13. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program. Here, the array index starts at 1."},{"type":"context","text":"The procedure maximumSubarray calculates the maximum sum of the subarray of the array\n T (the number of elements ≥ 1). A subarray is a contiguous portion of the array, for instance,\n from T[1] to T[3]. It can be as short as one element or as long as the entire array. The\n procedure finds one of the subarrays with the largest sum of values, and outputs the sum, the\n first, and the last indices of the subarray. For instance, if the content of the array is {-2, 1,\n -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4}, the output will be 6, 4, and 7 representing the sum, first index,\n and last index, respectively. Here, the subarray from T[4] to T[7] is {4, -1, 2, 1}."},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ maximumSubarray(integer []: T)\n  integer: n ← the number of elements in T\n  integer: i, j\n  integer: first, last  /* the first and last indices of the subarray */\n  integer: sum\n  integer: max ← T[1] - 1\n\n  for (increase i from 1 to n by 1)\n    sum ← 0\n    for (increase j from ***A*** to n by 1)\n      sum ← sum + T[j]\n      if (sum > max)\n        first ← i\n        last ← ***B***\n        max ← sum\n      endif\n    endfor\n  endfor\n\n  output max, first, last"},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"1","B":"j"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"1","B":"j - 1"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"i","B":"j"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"i","B":"j - 1"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"i + 1","B":"j"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"i + 1","B":"j - 1"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"1 j","values":{"A":"1","B":"j"}},{"label":"b","text":"1 j - 1","values":{"A":"1","B":"j - 1"}},{"label":"c","text":"i j","values":{"A":"i","B":"j"}},{"label":"d","text":"i j - 1","values":{"A":"i","B":"j - 1"}},{"label":"e","text":"i + 1 j","values":{"A":"i + 1","B":"j"}},{"label":"f","text":"i + 1 j - 1","values":{"A":"i + 1","B":"j - 1"}}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'The procedure tries every subarray by fixing the start i and extending the end j, so the inner loop must start at j = i (A) and sum accumulates T[i] through T[j]. When a new maximum is found, the subarray runs from i to the current j, so B is j. Starting j at i + 1 would skip single-element subarrays and drop T[i] from the sum.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-B, Q13)',
  ARRAY[21,22]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q13. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ____A____ and ____B____ in the program. Here, the array index starts at 1.

 The procedure maximumSubarray calculates the maximum sum of the subarray of the array
 T (the number of elements ≥ 1). A subarray is a contiguous portion of the array, for instance,
 from T[1] to T[3]. It can be as short as one element or as long as the entire array. The
 procedure finds one of the subarrays with the largest sum of values, and outputs the sum, the
 first, and the last indices of the subarray. For instance, if the content of the array is {-2, 1,
 -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4}, the output will be 6, 4, and 7 representing the sum, first index,
 and last index, respectively. Here, the subarray from T[4] to T[7] is {4, -1, 2, 1}.

 [Program]
 ○ maximumSubarray(integer []: T)
 integer: n ← the number of elements in T
 integer: i, j
 integer: first, last /* the first and last indices of the subarray */
 integer: sum
 integer: max ← T[1] - 1

 for (increase i from 1 to n by 1)
 sum ← 0
 for (increase j from ____A____ to n by 1)
 sum ← sum + T[j]
 if (sum > max)
 first ← i
 last ← ____B____
 max ← sum
 endif
 endfor
 endfor

 output max, first, last

Answer group
 A B
 a) 1 j
 b) 1 j - 1
 c) i j
 d) i j - 1
 e) i + 1 j
 f) i + 1 j - 1',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/questions.pdf","pages":[21,22]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-april',
  'B',
  14,
  'Q14. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program.

The function cosine(z) returns an approximate value of the cosine value of z degrees. The
 function uses Maclaurin series expansion for the cosine shown below and can be used to
 determine cos(y) for values of y radians.

cos(y) = 1 − (y²)/(2!) + (y⁴)/(4!) − (y⁶)/(6!) + ⋯

',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q14. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program."},{"type":"context","text":"The function cosine(z) returns an approximate value of the cosine value of z degrees. The\n function uses Maclaurin series expansion for the cosine shown below and can be used to\n determine cos(y) for values of y radians.\n\ncos(y) = 1 − (y²)/(2!) + (y⁴)/(4!) − (y⁶)/(6!) + ⋯\n\n"},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ real: cosine(real: z)\n  real: y ← z × π ÷ 180 // convert from degrees to radians\n  real: term ← 1\n  real: cosy ← term\n  integer: n ← 0\n  while (absolute value of ***A*** > 0.000000001)\n    n ← n + 1\n    term ← term × (-1 × (y ***B***) ÷ (2 × n × (2 × n - 1)))\n    cosy ← cosy + term\n  endwhile\n  return cosy\n\nAnswer group"},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"cosy","B":"raised to the power of (2 × n)"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"cosy","B":"raised to the power of 4"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"cosy","B":"squared"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"term","B":"raised to the power of (2 × n)"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"term","B":"raised to the power of 4"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"term","B":"squared"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"cosy raised to the power of (2 × n)","values":{"A":"cosy","B":"raised to the power of (2 × n)"}},{"label":"b","text":"cosy raised to the power of 4","values":{"A":"cosy","B":"raised to the power of 4"}},{"label":"c","text":"cosy squared","values":{"A":"cosy","B":"squared"}},{"label":"d","text":"term raised to the power of (2 × n)","values":{"A":"term","B":"raised to the power of (2 × n)"}},{"label":"e","text":"term raised to the power of 4","values":{"A":"term","B":"raised to the power of 4"}},{"label":"f","text":"term squared","values":{"A":"term","B":"squared"}}]'::jsonb,
  'f',
  '{"value":"f"}'::jsonb,
  'Each Maclaurin term is obtained from the previous one by multiplying by −y² ÷ (2n × (2n − 1)), for example 1 → −y²/2! → +y⁴/4!, so B is squared. The loop should stop when the newest term becomes negligible, so A is the absolute value of term compared with 0.000000001. Testing cosy would not work because cos(y) itself does not shrink toward 0, and using y raised to (2 × n) would multiply in far too large a power.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-B, Q14)',
  ARRAY[23]::int[],
  'manual_review',
  '3.0',
  'Q14. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ____A____ and ____B____ in the program.

 The function cosine(z) returns an approximate value of the cosine value of z degrees. The
 function uses Maclaurin series expansion for the cosine shown below and can be used to
 determine cos(y) for values of y radians.

 ௬మ ௬ర ௬ల
 cos(y) = 1 − + − + ⋯
 ଶ! ସ! ଺!

 [Program]
 ○ real: cosine(real: z)
 real: y ← z × π ÷ 180 // convert from degrees to radians
 real: term ← 1
 real: cosy ← term
 integer: n ← 0
 while (absolute value of ____A____ > 0.000000001)
 n ← n + 1
 term ← term × (-1 × (y ____B____) ÷ (2 × n × (2 × n - 1)))
 cosy ← cosy + term
 endwhile
 return cosy

 Answer group
 A B
 a) cosy raised to the power of (2 × n)
 b) cosy raised to the power of 4
 c) cosy squared
 d) term raised to the power of (2 × n)
 e) term raised to the power of 4
 f) term squared',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/questions.pdf","pages":[23]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-april/b/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
