  15,
  'Q15. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** and ***B*** in the description. Here, the array index starts at 1.

The calcDistance returns a value based on array p1, p2 and positive integer n. Assuming
 that the number of elements in arrays p1 and p2 are the same. The function abs(x) returns
 the absolute value of x, and pow(a, b) returns a raised to the power of b.
 In the case of p1 is {3, 1, 5, 2} and p2 is {4, 6, 2, 3}, the calcDistance(p1, p2,
 1) returns ***A***, whereas calcDistance(p1, p2, 2) returns ***B***. As the
 value of n increases, those of calcDistance(p1, p2, n) converges to 5.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q15. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** and ***B*** in the description. Here, the array index starts at 1."},{"type":"context","text":"The calcDistance returns a value based on array p1, p2 and positive integer n. Assuming\n that the number of elements in arrays p1 and p2 are the same. The function abs(x) returns\n the absolute value of x, and pow(a, b) returns a raised to the power of b.\n In the case of p1 is {3, 1, 5, 2} and p2 is {4, 6, 2, 3}, the calcDistance(p1, p2,\n 1) returns ***A***, whereas calcDistance(p1, p2, 2) returns ***B***. As the\n value of n increases, those of calcDistance(p1, p2, n) converges to 5."},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ real: calcDistance(real []: p1, real []: p2, integer: n)\n  integer: i\n  real: distance ← 0\n  real: ex\n  for (increase i from 1 to number of elements in p1 by 1)\n    distance ← distance + pow(abs(p1[i] - p2[i]), n)\n  endfor\n  ex ← 1 ÷ n /* Division is performed in data type real */\n  distance ← pow(distance, ex)\n  return distance"},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"4","B":"4"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"4","B":"6"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"4","B":"10"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"4","B":"18"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"10","B":"4"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"10","B":"6"}},{"label":"g","values":{"A":"10","B":"10"}},{"label":"h","values":{"A":"10","B":"18"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"4 4","values":{"A":"4","B":"4"}},{"label":"b","text":"4 6","values":{"A":"4","B":"6"}},{"label":"c","text":"4 10","values":{"A":"4","B":"10"}},{"label":"d","text":"4 18","values":{"A":"4","B":"18"}},{"label":"e","text":"10 4","values":{"A":"10","B":"4"}},{"label":"f","text":"10 6","values":{"A":"10","B":"6"}},{"label":"g","text":"10 10","values":{"A":"10","B":"10"}},{"label":"h","text":"10 18","values":{"A":"10","B":"18"}}]'::jsonb,
  'f',
  '{"value":"f"}'::jsonb,
  'The function computes the Minkowski (Lp) distance: (∑|p1[i] − p2[i]|^n)^(1/n). The differences are 1, 5, 3, 1, so for n = 1 the result is 1 + 5 + 3 + 1 = 10 (A). For n = 2 it is √(1 + 25 + 9 + 1) = √36 = 6 (B), and as n grows the value approaches the largest difference, 5.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025S, FE, Subject-B, Q15)',
  ARRAY[24]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q15. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ___A___ and ___B___ in the description. Here, the array index starts at 1.

 The calcDistance returns a value based on array p1, p2 and positive integer n. Assuming
 that the number of elements in arrays p1 and p2 are the same. The function abs(x) returns
 the absolute value of x, and pow(a, b) returns a raised to the power of b.
 In the case of p1 is {3, 1, 5, 2} and p2 is {4, 6, 2, 3}, the calcDistance(p1, p2,
 1) returns ___A___, whereas calcDistance(p1, p2, 2) returns ___B___. As the
 value of n increases, those of calcDistance(p1, p2, n) converges to 5.

 [Program]
 ○ real: calcDistance(real []: p1, real []: p2, integer: n)
 integer: i
 real: distance ← 0
 real: ex
 for (increase i from 1 to number of elements in p1 by 1)
 distance ← distance + pow(abs(p1[i] - p2[i]), n)
 endfor
 ex ← 1 ÷ n /* Division is performed in data type real */
 distance ← pow(distance, ex)
 return distance

Answer group
 A B
 a) 4 4
 b) 4 6
 c) 4 10
 d) 4 18
 e) 10 4
 f) 10 6
 g) 10 10
 h) 10 18',
