  'Q16. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ***A*** through ***C*** in the program.

Craps is a casino dice game in which players bet on the outcomes of the roll of a pair dice.
 In the rules of the game, a player rolls two dice and finds their sum. There possible outcomes
 could lead to a win or loss.

1. If the sum is 7 or 11, the player wins.
 2. If the sum is 2, 3 or 12, the player loses.
 3. Otherwise (the sum is 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 or 10), the player neither wins nor loses. However,
 the player continues rolling the dice until they either roll the same (initial) sum
 again (in which case they win the game) or roll a sum of 7 (in which case they lose
 the game).

The following program approximates the chance of winning a game of Craps using Monte
 Carlo method and outputs it. The Monte Carlo method is a technique used to estimate the
 probability of certain outcomes of an experiment by running multiple trial runs, using
 random numbers. In the program, the playing Craps is illustrated simply by generating
 random numbers rather than actually rolling a pair of dice. The function random_int(1, 6)
 generates a random integer number between 1 and 6, and returns it.
 The program also calculates and outputs the relative error of the measured approximate
 probability of winning Craps. In general, the relative error Er is calculated using the
 following formula:

Er = | (Pm – Pt) / Pt |

where Pm denotes the measured approximate probability and Pt denotes the theoretical
 probability. The theoretical probability of winning Craps is known to be 244/495.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q16. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** through ***C*** in the program."},{"type":"context","text":"Craps is a casino dice game in which players bet on the outcomes of the roll of a pair dice.\n In the rules of the game, a player rolls two dice and finds their sum. There possible outcomes\n could lead to a win or loss.\n\n1. If the sum is 7 or 11, the player wins.\n 2. If the sum is 2, 3 or 12, the player loses.\n 3. Otherwise (the sum is 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 or 10), the player neither wins nor loses. However,\n the player continues rolling the dice until they either roll the same (initial) sum\n again (in which case they win the game) or roll a sum of 7 (in which case they lose\n the game).\n\nThe following program approximates the chance of winning a game of Craps using Monte\n Carlo method and outputs it. The Monte Carlo method is a technique used to estimate the\n probability of certain outcomes of an experiment by running multiple trial runs, using\n random numbers. In the program, the playing Craps is illustrated simply by generating\n random numbers rather than actually rolling a pair of dice. The function random_int(1, 6)\n generates a random integer number between 1 and 6, and returns it.\n The program also calculates and outputs the relative error of the measured approximate\n probability of winning Craps. In general, the relative error Er is calculated using the\n following formula:\n\nEr = | (Pm – Pt) / Pt |\n\nwhere Pm denotes the measured approximate probability and Pt denotes the theoretical\n probability. The theoretical probability of winning Craps is known to be 244/495."},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"integer: wins_sum ← 0\ninteger: lose_sum ← 0\ninteger: n ← 10000\ninteger: i, dice1, dice2, sum, newsum\nreal: result, pt ← (244 ÷ 495)\nfor (increase i from 1 to n by 1)\n  dice1 ← random_int(1, 6)\n  dice2 ← random_int(1, 6)\n  sum ← dice1 + dice2\n  if (sum = 7 or sum = 11)\nwins_sum ← wins_sum + 1\n  elseif (sum = 2 or sum = 3 or sum = 12)\n    lose_sum ← lose_sum + 1\n  else\n    do\n      dice1 ← random_int(1, 6)\n      dice2 ← random_int(1, 6)\n      newsum ← dice1 + dice2\n      if (newsum = sum)\n        wins_sum ← wins_sum + 1\n      elseif (newsum = 7)\n        lose_sum ← lose_sum + 1\n      endif\n    while (***A***)\n  endif\nendfor\nresult ← ***B***\noutput result, absolute value of (***C***)\n\nAnswer group"},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B","C"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum and\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ lose sum\n_ _","C":"(result − pt) ÷ pt"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum and\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ lose sum\n_ _","C":"result ÷ pt"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum and\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ n\n_","C":"(result − pt) ÷ pt"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum and\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ n\n_","C":"result ÷ pt"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum or\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ lose sum\n_ _","C":"(result − pt) ÷ pt"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum or\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ lose sum\n_ _","C":"result ÷ pt"}},{"label":"g","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum or\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ n\n_","C":"(result − pt) ÷ pt"}},{"label":"h","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum or\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ n\n_","C":"result ÷ pt"}}]}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"newsum ≠ 7\n\n newsum ≠ sum and wins_sum ÷ lose_sum result ÷ pt","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum and\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ lose sum\n_ _","C":"(result − pt) ÷ pt"}},{"label":"b","text":"newsum ≠ 7\n\n newsum ≠ sum and wins_sum ÷ n (result − pt) ÷ pt","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum and\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ lose sum\n_ _","C":"result ÷ pt"}},{"label":"c","text":"newsum ≠ 7\n\n newsum ≠ sum and wins_sum ÷ n result ÷ pt","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum and\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ n\n_","C":"(result − pt) ÷ pt"}},{"label":"d","text":"newsum ≠ 7\n\n newsum ≠ sum or wins_sum ÷ lose_sum (result − pt) ÷ pt","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum and\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ n\n_","C":"result ÷ pt"}},{"label":"e","text":"newsum ≠ 7\n\n newsum ≠ sum or wins_sum ÷ lose_sum result ÷ pt","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum or\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ lose sum\n_ _","C":"(result − pt) ÷ pt"}},{"label":"f","text":"newsum ≠ 7\n\n newsum ≠ sum or wins_sum ÷ n (result − pt) ÷ pt","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum or\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ lose sum\n_ _","C":"result ÷ pt"}},{"label":"g","text":"newsum ≠ 7\n\n newsum ≠ sum or wins_sum ÷ n result ÷ pt","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum or\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ n\n_","C":"(result − pt) ÷ pt"}},{"label":"h","text":"newsum ≠ 7","values":{"A":"newsum ≠ sum or\nnewsum ≠ 7","B":"wins sum ÷ n\n_","C":"result ÷ pt"}}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'In the point phase the player keeps rolling while the new sum is neither the point nor 7, so A is newsum ≠ sum and newsum ≠ 7; with or the loop would never stop, because newsum cannot equal both the point and 7. The estimated probability of winning is wins divided by the number of games played, so B is wins_sum ÷ n (wins ÷ losses is an odds ratio, not a probability). C follows the relative error formula (Pm − Pt) ÷ Pt, written (result − pt) ÷ pt.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
