  18,
  'Q18. From the answer group below, select the most appropriate combination of answers to be
 inserted into ***A*** and ***B*** in the description.

Company Z wants to provide secure file transfer service especially for very large files for
 their customers. Mr. K, the system architect of the company, designed the sequence of
 uploading, storing, and downloading customer files on the web service. In this system,
 customers register their email addresses and their public keys during sign up. The associated
 private keys reside on the customers’ computers and the email address is used as the user id.
 The requirements for the file transfer service are as follows:

- Customers transfer files between each other by uploading files to or downloading them
 from the web service provided by Company Z.
 - When uploading a file, the sender supplies the recipient’s email address and the
 password that will be used with the particular file (Upload function).
 - The file uploaded by the sender is encrypted with the supplied password and then stored
 in the database of the web service. (StoreFile function).
 - The web service assigns a file id to the uploaded file and notifies the sender of it upon
 uploading.
 - The encrypted file can only be decrypted by the sender and the recipient of the file using
 the password supplied by the sender.
 - The password is encrypted and sent to the recipient as an email message along with the
 file id (Message function).
 - When downloading the file, the recipient supplies the file id and the password. The web
 service then decrypts the stored file and provides it for the recipient.
 - Even if the database is leaked, it will be difficult to decrypt the uploaded files stored in
 the database.

Figure 1 shows the sequence of uploading file by customer P for customer Q.

P Server Database Q

Upload(Q’s email, file, password)

LoadPublicKeys(Q’s email)

Q’s public key

StoreFile( A (file, KDF(password)))

file id

Message(file id, B (password, Q’s public key))
 file id

Figure 1 Sequence on file uploading

In this system, Mr.K uses KDF (Key Derivation Function) to improve the strength of the
supplied password and uses ***A*** and ***B*** algorithms to accomplish above
requirements. Note that the format of encryption algorithms used here is algorithm(data,
key).',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q18. From the answer group below, select the most appropriate combination of answers to be\n inserted into ***A*** and ***B*** in the description."},{"type":"context","text":"Company Z wants to provide secure file transfer service especially for very large files for\n their customers. Mr. K, the system architect of the company, designed the sequence of\n uploading, storing, and downloading customer files on the web service. In this system,\n customers register their email addresses and their public keys during sign up. The associated\n private keys reside on the customers’ computers and the email address is used as the user id.\n The requirements for the file transfer service are as follows:\n\n- Customers transfer files between each other by uploading files to or downloading them\n from the web service provided by Company Z.\n - When uploading a file, the sender supplies the recipient’s email address and the\n password that will be used with the particular file (Upload function).\n - The file uploaded by the sender is encrypted with the supplied password and then stored\n in the database of the web service. (StoreFile function).\n - The web service assigns a file id to the uploaded file and notifies the sender of it upon\n uploading.\n - The encrypted file can only be decrypted by the sender and the recipient of the file using\n the password supplied by the sender.\n - The password is encrypted and sent to the recipient as an email message along with the\n file id (Message function).\n - When downloading the file, the recipient supplies the file id and the password. The web\n service then decrypts the stored file and provides it for the recipient.\n - Even if the database is leaked, it will be difficult to decrypt the uploaded files stored in\n the database.\n\nFigure 1 shows the sequence of uploading file by customer P for customer Q.\n\nP Server Database Q\n\nUpload(Q’s email, file, password)\n\nLoadPublicKeys(Q’s email)\n\nQ’s public key\n\nStoreFile( A (file, KDF(password)))\n\nfile id\n\nMessage(file id, B (password, Q’s public key))\n file id\n\nFigure 1 Sequence on file uploading\n\nIn this system, Mr.K uses KDF (Key Derivation Function) to improve the strength of the\nsupplied password and uses ***A*** and ***B*** algorithms to accomplish above\nrequirements. Note that the format of encryption algorithms used here is algorithm(data,\nkey)."},{"type":"answer_table","columns":["A","B"],"rows":[{"label":"a","values":{"A":"asymmetric key encryption","B":"cryptographically secure hash"}},{"label":"b","values":{"A":"asymmetric key encryption","B":"symmetric key encryption"}},{"label":"c","values":{"A":"cryptographically secure hash","B":"asymmetric key encryption"}},{"label":"d","values":{"A":"cryptographically secure hash","B":"symmetric key encryption"}},{"label":"e","values":{"A":"symmetric key encryption","B":"asymmetric key encryption"}},{"label":"f","values":{"A":"symmetric key encryption","B":"cryptographically secure hash"}}]}]'::jsonb,
