  '[{"label":"a","text":"asymmetric key encryption cryptographically secure hash","values":{"A":"asymmetric key encryption","B":"cryptographically secure hash"}},{"label":"b","text":"asymmetric key encryption symmetric key encryption","values":{"A":"asymmetric key encryption","B":"symmetric key encryption"}},{"label":"c","text":"cryptographically secure hash asymmetric key encryption","values":{"A":"cryptographically secure hash","B":"asymmetric key encryption"}},{"label":"d","text":"cryptographically secure hash symmetric key encryption","values":{"A":"cryptographically secure hash","B":"symmetric key encryption"}},{"label":"e","text":"symmetric key encryption asymmetric key encryption","values":{"A":"symmetric key encryption","B":"asymmetric key encryption"}},{"label":"f","text":"symmetric key encryption cryptographically secure hash","values":{"A":"symmetric key encryption","B":"cryptographically secure hash"}}]'::jsonb,
  'e',
  '{"value":"e"}'::jsonb,
  'A is symmetric key encryption: the large file is encrypted with a single key derived from the password by KDF, and the same password-derived key decrypts it for download, which is fast for big files. B is asymmetric key encryption: the password is encrypted with Q''s public key so that only Q, holding the matching private key, can read it. A hash cannot be used for either, because hashes are one-way and the file and password must be recoverable.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'cryptography'),
