(
  '2025-october',
  'A',
  6,
  'Q6. The flowchart shown below expresses an algorithm that determines the greatest common divisor between two integers a and b. How many times is A passed?',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q6. The flowchart shown below expresses an algorithm that determines the greatest common divisor between two integers a and b. How many times is A passed?"}]'::jsonb,
  '["2025-october-a-q06-figure1.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"1"},{"label":"b","text":"2"},{"label":"c","text":"4"},{"label":"d","text":"8"}]'::jsonb,
  'b',
  '{"value":"b"}'::jsonb,
  'The official answer is 2. With a = 56 and b = 48, the first pass computes r = 8 (then a = 48, b = 8) and the second pass computes r = 0 (then a = 8, b = 0), after which the GCD 8 is printed. The loop that starts at the merge point labelled A is therefore executed twice, which is the count the official answer uses. Note that the return line itself is taken only once (after r = 8), so the answer depends on reading A as the entry to the loop.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025A, FE, Subject-A, Q6)',
  ARRAY[6]::int[],
  'manual_review',
  '3.0',
  'Q6. The flowchart shown below expresses an algorithm that determines the greatest common
 divisor between two integers a and b. How many times is A passed?

 Start

 a ← 56
 b ← 48

 r ← a mod b

 A
 a ← b
 b ← r

 No
 r = 0
 Yes
 print a

 End

 a) 1 b) 2 c) 4 d) 8',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-october/a/questions.pdf","pages":[6]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-october/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-october',
  'A',
  7,
  'Q7. In object-oriented programming, which of the following is the concept that refers to a class
 that defines a method with the same name and type signature as a method in its superclass?',
  '[{"type":"text","page":6,"bbox":[65.22,435.65,533.16,466.93],"text":"Q7.  In object-oriented programming, which of the following is the concept that refers to a class"},{"type":"text","page":6,"bbox":[85.74,471.65,525.84,484.93],"text":"that defines a method with the same name and type signature as a method in its superclass?"},{"type":"text","page":6,"bbox":[84.12,507.65,391.24,538.93],"text":"a) Hiding\nb)  Inheritance\nc) Overloading\nd)  Overriding"},{"type":"code","page":6,"bbox":[65.22,794.63,311.58,807.97],"text":"– 6 –"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"Hiding"},{"label":"b","text":"Inheritance"},{"label":"c","text":"Overloading"},{"label":"d","text":"Overriding"}]'::jsonb,
  'd',
  '{"value":"d"}'::jsonb,
  'The answer is overriding: a subclass redefines a method that has the same name and the same signature as a method in its superclass, replacing the inherited behaviour. Overloading is different: it defines several methods with the same name but different parameter lists, usually in the same class. Inheritance is the general mechanism that lets the subclass receive the method in the first place.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'software-development'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025A, FE, Subject-A, Q7)',
  ARRAY[6]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q7. In object-oriented programming, which of the following is the concept that refers to a class
 that defines a method with the same name and type signature as a method in its superclass?

 a) Hiding b) Inheritance
 c) Overloading d) Overriding',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-october/a/questions.pdf","pages":[6]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-october/a/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
  true
),
(
  '2025-october',
  'A',
  8,
  'Q8. There exists a main process and the two (2) interrupt processes A and B, as shown in the table below. Multiplex interrupts are permitted. When interrupts A and B occur with the timing shown in the diagram below, how many milliseconds (ms) can the main process use the CPU during the time period between 0 ms and 5 ms? Here, the overhead from calling an interrupt process and resuming from it can be ignored.',
  '[{"type":"text","text":"Q8. There exists a main process and the two (2) interrupt processes A and B, as shown in the table below. Multiplex interrupts are permitted. When interrupts A and B occur with the timing shown in the diagram below, how many milliseconds (ms) can the main process use the CPU during the time period between 0 ms and 5 ms? Here, the overhead from calling an interrupt process and resuming from it can be ignored."}]'::jsonb,
  '["2025-october-a-q08-figure1.webp"]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"2"},{"label":"b","text":"2.5"},{"label":"c","text":"3.5"},{"label":"d","text":"5"}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'The main process gets 2 ms. B starts at 0 ms and runs 1 ms until the higher-priority A interrupts at 1 ms; A runs 1–1.5 ms, then B finishes its remaining 0.5 ms at 1.5–2 ms. The main process then runs 2–2.5, 3–3.5 and 4–5 ms, while A runs 2.5–3 and 3.5–4 ms, so the main process uses 0.5 + 0.5 + 1 = 2 ms (the B interrupt at 5 ms falls outside the period).',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'operating-systems'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025A, FE, Subject-A, Q8)',
  ARRAY[7]::int[],
  'manual_review',
  '3.0',
  'Q8. There exists a main process and the two (2) interrupt processes A and B, as shown in the
 table below. Multiplex interrupts are permitted. When interrupts A and B occur with the
