  '[{"label":"a","text":"“1”, “2”, “3”, “4”, “5”"},{"label":"b","text":"“1”, “2”, “3”, “5”, “4”"},{"label":"c","text":"“1”, “2”, “4”, “3”, “5”"},{"label":"d","text":"“1”, “2”, “4”, “5”, “3”"},{"label":"e","text":"“1”, “2”, “5”, “4”, “3”"},{"label":"f","text":"“1”, “4”, “5”, “2”, “3”"}]'::jsonb,
  'c',
  '{"value":"c"}'::jsonb,
  'The output order is 1, 2, 4, 3, 5, because the program is a breadth-first search using a queue. Starting from 1, its unvisited neighbours 2 and 4 are enqueued in index order; dequeuing 2 adds its unvisited neighbours 3 and 5, and dequeuing 4 adds nothing new. The queue therefore yields 1, 2, 4, 3, 5; an order such as 1, 2, 3, 5, 4 would come from a depth-first (stack or recursive) traversal.',
