The bubble sort compares adjacent elements in an array and swaps them if they are out of
 order. It makes multiple passes through an array. The bubble sort can be modified to stop
 early if it finds that the array has become sorted. The function quickBubble is modified
 from conventional bubble sort, which sorts the elements in ascending order, to recognize a
 sorted array and stop early. For example, the sorting of the unordered array {18, 1, 8, 6,
 2, 9, 12, 14, 7, 11} is completed in six passes.',
  '[{"type":"prompt","text":"Q11. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted\n into ***A*** and ***B*** in the program. Here, the array index starts at 1."},{"type":"context","text":"The bubble sort compares adjacent elements in an array and swaps them if they are out of\n order. It makes multiple passes through an array. The bubble sort can be modified to stop\n early if it finds that the array has become sorted. The function quickBubble is modified\n from conventional bubble sort, which sorts the elements in ascending order, to recognize a\n sorted array and stop early. For example, the sorting of the unordered array {18, 1, 8, 6,\n 2, 9, 12, 14, 7, 11} is completed in six passes."},{"type":"code","language":"itpec-pseudocode","text":"○ integer []: quickBubble(integer []: array)\n  integer []: arraySorted ← array\n  boolean: exchange\n  integer: maxIdx, i\n  exchange ← true\n  maxIdx ← (the number of elements in arraySorted) - 1\n  while (***A*** and ***B***)\n    exchange ← false\n    for (increase i from 1 to maxIdx by 1)\n      if (arraySorted[i] > arraySorted[i + 1])\n        exchange ← true\n        swap the values of arraySorted[i] and arraySorted[i + 1]\n      endif\n    endfor\n    maxIdx ← maxIdx - 1\n  endwhile\n  return arraySorted"}]'::jsonb,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  '[{"label":"a","text":"maxIdx > 0 exchange = true"},{"label":"b","text":"maxIdx > 0 exchange = false"},{"label":"c","text":"maxIdx > 1 exchange = true"},{"label":"d","text":"maxIdx > 1 exchange = false"}]'::jsonb,
  'a',
  '{"value":"a"}'::jsonb,
  'A is maxIdx > 0 and B is exchange = true. maxIdx is the last index compared in a pass, and a pass is still meaningful as long as it is at least 1 (comparing elements 1 and 2), so the loop runs while maxIdx > 0; maxIdx > 1 would skip that final comparison. The early stop works by continuing only while the previous pass swapped something; exchange starts as true so the first pass runs, and a pass with no swaps means the array is sorted.',
  (SELECT id FROM subtopics WHERE slug = 'algorithms'),
  'medium',
  0.5,
  'questions.pdf',
  '(2025A, FE, Subject-B, Q11)',
  ARRAY[20]::int[],
  'native_text',
  '3.0',
  'Q11. From the answer group below, select the correct combination of answers to be inserted
 into ____A____ and ____B____ in the program. Here, the array index starts at 1.

 The bubble sort compares adjacent elements in an array and swaps them if they are out of
 order. It makes multiple passes through an array. The bubble sort can be modified to stop
 early if it finds that the array has become sorted. The function quickBubble is modified
 from conventional bubble sort, which sorts the elements in ascending order, to recognize a
 sorted array and stop early. For example, the sorting of the unordered array {18, 1, 8, 6,
 2, 9, 12, 14, 7, 11} is completed in six passes.

 [Program]
 ○ integer []: quickBubble(integer []: array)
 integer []: arraySorted ← array
 boolean: exchange
 integer: maxIdx, i
 exchange ← true
 maxIdx ← (the number of elements in arraySorted) - 1
 while (___A___ and ___B___)
 exchange ← false
 for (increase i from 1 to maxIdx by 1)
 if (arraySorted[i] > arraySorted[i + 1])
 exchange ← true
 swap the values of arraySorted[i] and arraySorted[i + 1]
 endif
 endfor
 maxIdx ← maxIdx - 1
 endwhile
 return arraySorted

 Answer group
 A B
 a) maxIdx > 0 exchange = true
 b) maxIdx > 0 exchange = false
 c) maxIdx > 1 exchange = true
 d) maxIdx > 1 exchange = false',
  '{"question_pdf":{"filename":"questions.pdf","storage_path":"2025-october/b/questions.pdf","pages":[20]},"answer_pdf":{"filename":"answers.pdf","storage_path":"2025-october/b/answers.pdf","pages":[1]}}'::jsonb,
